[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/pz_ib]
In 1935, a commander tank designated Kleiner Panzerbefehlswagen I A (also known as Sd.Kfz.265) was designed from the Pz.Kpfw.I Ausf.A. The turret was replaced with a superstructure which could hold two crew members (a commander and a radio operator) and two radio stations. Six such vehicles were produced in late 1935.
In 1936, an upgraded version of the tank was designed based on the Pz.Kpfw.I Ausf.B chassis. Apart from the new chassis the tank designated Kleiner Panzerbefehlswagen I B differed in its standard armament (one MG-34 7.92 mm machine gun) and a redesigned superstructure. Two series of the Kleiner Panzerbefehlswagen I B were produced with a total number of 200 vehicles. The tanks were upgraded before the Polish campaign of 1939 and received a FuG 8 radio station, the frame aerial being replaced by a stub antenna. 
Like most commander vehicles, the Kleiner Panzerbefehlswagen I B should not be treated as a front combat unit. Commander tanks were supposed to stay back and to coordinate the tank groups, while their own armament provided protection only against enemy infantry.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/klpzbfwg_i]
In 1935, a commander tank designated Kleiner Panzerbefehlswagen I A (also known as Sd.Kfz.265) was designed from the Pz.Kpfw.I Ausf.A. The turret was replaced with a superstructure which could hold two crew members (a commander and a radio operator) and two radio stations. Six such vehicles were produced in late 1935.
In 1936, an upgraded version of the tank was designed based on the Pz.Kpfw.I Ausf.B chassis. Apart from the new chassis the tank designated Kleiner Panzerbefehlswagen I B differed in its standard armament (one MG-34 7.92 mm machine gun) and a redesigned superstructure. Two series of the Kleiner Panzerbefehlswagen I B were produced with a total number of 200 vehicles. The tanks were upgraded before the Polish campaign of 1939 and received a FuG 8 radio station, the frame aerial being replaced by a stub antenna. 
Like most commander vehicles, the Kleiner Panzerbefehlswagen I B should not be treated as a front combat unit. Commander tanks were supposed to stay back and to coordinate the tank groups, while their own armament provided protection only against enemy infantry.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/pz_iic]
In late 1934 the Army Ordnance Department ordered the development of a light tank weighing 10 tons and equipped with a 20 mm gun. Since the Treaty of Versailles prohibited Germany from developing and producing tanks, the project was designated La.S 100 (Landwirtschaftlicher Schlepper 100, i.e. an agricultural tractor 100). Four firms took part in the contest for the development of the La.S 100, and the joint design by MAN and Daimler-Benz was chosen.
The first ten tanks of this type were produced in late 1935. By that time they were designated Panzerkampfwagen II Ausfuehrung A (abbreviated as Pz.Kpfw.II Ausf.A) and the Wehrmacht’s official designation Sd.Kfz.121. Three series of the Pz.Kpfw.II Ausf.A were produced as well as a Pz.Kpfw.II Ausf.B version, which was slightly different from the others. The Pz.Kpfw.II Ausf.C, which had a different running gear, was produced in large numbers; a total of 1,113 Pz.Kpfw.II Ausf.C tanks were produced.
The Pz.Kpfw. II was primarily intended for training the crews. Nevertheless, the Pz.Kpfw.II was used by the troops until 1943 and unlike the smaller Pz.Kpfw.I it kept a fighting chance. When the Pz.Kpfw.II Ausf.C appeared it was already inferior in armamentto such tanks as the 7TP and T-26 designed as of 1933, but it could still fight against them. The German tank was several times more maneuverable, which was a decisive factor in more than one case.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_IIF]
Panzerkampfwagen II Ausf.F Light Tank

The Pz.II Ausf F was the last mass-produced Panzer II variant. Lessons learned in the course of fighting in Poland and France were applied to its design, which led most importantly to the thickening of its frontal armour to 30-35mm. The FAMO company began mass production of the new variant in March of 1941. Production ended in December of 1942, after a total of 524 Ausf.Fs had been produced.

Tanks of this type saw active service on the Eastern Front in 1941-1942, and in North Africa during the same period. Light and manoeuvrable, this tank was perfectly suited for fast manoeuvres in the enemy rears. Its 20mm cannon performed very well, and proved quite capable of taking out light tanks and armoured cars, whose armour was only designed to stop small-arms fire. The Panzer II’s frontal armour could often stop a 45mm projectile, especially on the Eastern Front where the Soviet 45mm ammunition was plagued with difficulties throughout 1941.

Nevertheless, by early 1942 this model was clearly obsolete. The Russians had solved their 45mm projectile woes, and the Panzer II armour was no longer sufficient. The Red Army also had a large number of anti-tank rifles readily available, which could make Swiss cheese of the tank’s 15mm side armour. Increasingly, more T-34s began to appear on the front, against which the Pz.II was powerless. In North Africa the situation was equally dire. There, the new Allied tanks easily outclassed the Pz.II; even the American M3 Stuart, ostensibly in the same class as the Pz.II, outperformed it in every category.

As a result, in 1942 the Panzer II began to disappear from the front, instead being relegated to rear echelon guard duties. An increasing number of tank hulls were also used to create self-propelled guns, although for some reason production of the stock Pz.II continued until the end of 1942. By early 1943, few Pz.IIs remained in Africa, although they could still be seen from time to time. For instance, the 10th Panzer Division, sent to Tunisia in November of 1942, had 21 Pz.II Ausf.Fs. A few tanks of this type even participated in the Battle of Kursk. By 4th of July, 1943, German Army Group South had fifty-four Pz.IIs, including four with the Division Grossdeutschland. By July 31st, five of their Pz.IIs were written off as lost, all of which were from the 6th and 7th Panzer Divisions. Single examples of this model remained in service until the end of the war. By March of 1945, the Wehrmacht used fifteen Pz.IIs as front-line units, with 130 more in the reserves. The successor to the Ausf.F, the Panzer II Ausf.L, was produced in very small numbers, even though it had a completely different design.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/pz_35t]
In 1934, Skoda designed a SU light tank which was used as the basis for a new S-II-a. In the following year the tank was supplied to the Czechoslovakian Army under the designation LT vz.35. The tank was produced at the Skoda and CKD factories, and a total of 298 were produced.
After the infamous Munich Agreement Germany annexed the Czech territories and Moravia in March 1939, and the Germans received 218 LT vz.35 tanks, while Slovakia received another 79 tanks of this type. In the German Army the tank was designated as Pz.Kpfw.35(t).
The engine of the Pz.Kpfw.35(t) was mounted in the rear along with the transmission. Unlike German tanks, Czech tanks had riveted hulls and turrets. This made the assembly easier but if the tanks were hit by shells, the rivet heads would often break off and injure the crew. The primary armament of the Pz.Kpfw.35(t) was the 37 mm Skoda A-3 vz.34 gun (German designation 37 mm Kw.K 34(t)) located in the turret. Coupled with the gun was the 7.92 mm ZB vz.35 or ZB vz.37 coaxial machine gun (MG 35(t) and MG 37(t) respectively), and another machine gun was fitted in the front armor plate in a special ball mount. The running gear was characteristic of Skoda’s tanks and consisted of eight dual road wheels, which were blocked in pairs and assembled into two bogies with leaf springs. Another dual wheel was located between the front bogie and the idler. This design enabled smooth running. 
The Germans were using the Pz.Kpfw.35(t) from the Polish campaign until the winter of 1941. Having similar weight the Czech tank was superior to its German peer Pz.Kpfw.II almost in all parameters and was very similar to the early versions of the Pz.Kpfw.III. The Pz.Kpfw.35(t) was taken as a basis for the design of the Befehlswagen 35(t) commander tank and the artillery tractor – Artillerie Schlepper 35(t).

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/pz_38t]
The development of Pz.Kpfw.38(t) started in 1935 when the firm Ceskomoravska Kolben Danek (CKD) was hired by the Iranian Government to design a tank with a 37 mm gun and two machine guns. The tank designated Praga TNH was created in 1936, and Iran received about fifty tanks of this type. Based on this project CKD developed another tank Praga TNH-S intended for the Czechoslovak Army. After testing the prototype in 1937-38, the tank TNH-S entered service in August 1938 under the designation LT vz.38. Thus, CKD managed to beat its major competitor Skoda. A total of 10 LT vz.38(t) were produced by mid-March 1939. This vehicle was not only produced in Czechoslovakia but also by license in Sweden. The slightly re-designed Swedish tank was designated Strv m/41.
After Germany had occupied the Czech Republic, the LT vz.38 was almost immediately supplied to the German Army. The original vehicle was called L.T.M. 38 (Leichter Tank Muster 38, i.e. a light tank designed as of 1938) and it was only in 1940 that it received its designation Panzerkampfwagen 38 (t) abbreviated as Pz.Kpfw.38 (t). The German tanks did not differ from the LT vz.38 at first, except for the electric equipment and the German radio station installed there. The Pz.Kpfw.38 (t) was the only foreign tank not only supplied to the army but also produced in series after the occupation of the country. The tank production continued at CKD, which was renamed Bohmisch Mahriche Maschinenfabrik (BMM) after the occupation. A total of 475 tanks were produced from late May 1939 until November 1940 in four series (Ausf.A, Ausf.B, Ausf.C and Ausf.D). Each series had minor and sometimes hardly noticeable changes. For example, the tanks of the Ausf.C-D version had a thicker front armor plate (40 mm). In November 1940, the production of the Pz.Kpfw.38(t) Ausf.E tank began, which differed from its predecessors by a straight front armor plate and reinforced armor (with a hull and turret front of 50 mm and with hull and turret sides of 30 mm). The gross weight of this tank reached 9.87 tons, a large-size SPTA box was mounted on a shelf above the left track, and the suspension was reinforced. A total of 275 Pz.Kpfw.38(t) Ausf.E tanks were produced. Around 250 Pz.Kpfw.38(t) Ausf.F models were produced and it was almost identical with the previous model. The final major modification was the Pz.Kpfw.38(t) Ausf.G. BMM produced a total of 1,414 tanks of all modifications. A large number of various self-propelled artillery vehicles were produced on the basis of Pz.Kpfw.38(t). 
The fact that large-scale production of the LT vz.38 began after the Czech Republic had been occupied shows how much the Wehrmacht liked this tank. Upon creation this vehicle was the best light tank in the world, very closely related to the Pz.Kpfw.III Ausf.E in its features. This tank could not cope with the T-34 but they had different weight classes. The Pz.Kpfw.38(t) became obsolete by mid-1942.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/pz_38t_F]
The development of Pz.Kpfw.38(t) started in 1935 when the firm Ceskomoravska Kolben Danek (CKD) was hired by the Iranian Government to design a tank with a 37 mm gun and two machine guns. The tank designated Praga TNH was created in 1936, and Iran received about fifty tanks of this type. Based on this project CKD developed another tank Praga TNH-S intended for the Czechoslovak Army. After testing the prototype in 1937-38, the tank TNH-S entered service in August 1938 under the designation LT vz.38. Thus, CKD managed to beat its major competitor Skoda. A total of 10 LT vz.38(t) were produced by mid-March 1939. This vehicle was not only produced in Czechoslovakia but also by license in Sweden. The slightly re-designed Swedish tank was designated Strv m/41.
After Germany had occupied the Czech Republic, the LT vz.38 was almost immediately supplied to the German Army. The original vehicle was called L.T.M. 38 (Leichter Tank Muster 38, i.e. a light tank designed as of 1938) and it was only in 1940 that it received its designation Panzerkampfwagen 38 (t) abbreviated as Pz.Kpfw.38 (t). The German tanks did not differ from the LT vz.38 at first, except for the electric equipment and the German radio station installed there. The Pz.Kpfw.38 (t) was the only foreign tank not only supplied to the army but also produced in series after the occupation of the country. The tank production continued at CKD, which was renamed Bohmisch Mahriche Maschinenfabrik (BMM) after the occupation. A total of 475 tanks were produced from late May 1939 until November 1940 in four series (Ausf.A, Ausf.B, Ausf.C and Ausf.D). Each series had minor and sometimes hardly noticeable changes. For example, the tanks of the Ausf.C-D version had a thicker front armor plate (40 mm). In November 1940, the production of the Pz.Kpfw.38(t) Ausf.E tank began, which differed from its predecessors by a straight front armor plate and reinforced armor (with a hull and turret front of 50 mm and with hull and turret sides of 30 mm). The gross weight of this tank reached 9.87 tons, a large-size SPTA box was mounted on a shelf above the left track, and the suspension was reinforced. A total of 275 Pz.Kpfw.38(t) Ausf.E tanks were produced. Around 250 Pz.Kpfw.38(t) Ausf.F models were produced and it was almost identical with the previous model. The final major modification was the Pz.Kpfw.38(t) Ausf.G. BMM produced a total of 1,414 tanks of all modifications. A large number of various self-propelled artillery vehicles were produced on the basis of Pz.Kpfw.38(t). 
The fact that large-scale production of the LT vz.38 began after the Czech Republic had been occupied shows how much the Wehrmacht liked this tank. Upon creation this vehicle was the best light tank in the world, very closely related to the Pz.Kpfw.III Ausf.E in its features. This tank could not cope with the T-34 but they had different weight classes. The Pz.Kpfw.38(t) became obsolete by mid-1942.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/pz_iiie]
Fifth version of the Panzer III with 30 mm (1.2 in) armour all-round, other than the rear of the vehicle, which increased the weight to 20 t (22 short tons). Suspension redesigned, switching from leaf-springs to torsion-bars, now using six larger roadwheels per side. Had a 300 PS HL 120 engine.


[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/pz_iiif]
In 1934 the Army Ordnance Department ordered the development of a medium tank. The order required that the tank should have a combat weight of 15 tons and its armament should consist of a 37 mm gun and three 7.92 mm machine guns. The project was entitled Zugfuhrerwagen, i.e. squad commander vehicle. This name was chosen to keep the project a secret: in fact the Zugfuhrerwagen was developed as the main tank of the Reichswehr (and later the Wehrmacht). According to the organizational chart, each tank battalion was to include three squads of “squad commander vehicles.” 
Four firms took part in the contest for the development, and the design by Daimler-Benzwas finally chosen. By that time the tank was designated Panzerkampfwagen III and received the  official Wehrmacht designation Sd.Kfz.141. In 1936, 10 Pz.Kpfw.III Ausf.A tanks were produced, which were essentially trial vehicles. Their running gear featured five road wheels on either side, each wheel with an individual helical spring. The next two series, Ausf.B and Ausf.C (12 and 15 tanks respectively) featured a new running gear with eight road wheels on each side. The road wheels had leaf springs. In 1938 a new modification, Ausf.D, appeared and 30 such vehicles were produced. The tank still had a complex and expensive leaf spring suspension but this vehicle could was considered to be pretty much fit for combat conditions. The reinforced armor brought the tank weight up to 19.8 tons.
The Pz.Kpfw.III Ausf.E was the first real mass production model of the Pz.Kpfw.III series. This vehicle featured a torsion bar suspension designed by Ferdinand Porsche. The tank also had a new engine, two-piece side hatches installed in the turret, and a re-designed ball mount for the bow machine gun. The hull sides now had escape hatches. A total of 96 Pz.Kpfw.III Ausf.E tanks were produced. In September 1939 the Pz.Kpfw.III Ausf.F version entered mass production. This tank differed from the previous versions in air intakes added to the front armor plate to cool the brakes. A total of 435 tanks of this version were produced. Around 100 tanks were equipped with the 50 mm Kw.K 38 L/42  gun mounted in a new mantlet (while one of the coaxial machine guns was removed at the same time). Later on, all the tanks of the Ausf.E-F versions were rearmed in the same way.
Due to the long search for the best running gear there were fewer Pz.Kpfw.III in the army than Pz.Kpfw.IV, which were support tanks, by September 1939. The Pz.Kpfw.III became the principal German tank in 1940 and retained this status until late 1942. The armor of the first Pz.Kpfw.III models could be penetrated by almost all the types of the enemy anti-tank guns; but at that time there was not a single medium tank with shell-proof armor. The Pz.Kpfw.III had a number of advantages compared to enemy tanks: e.g. a perfect optical system and comfortable crew compartment. The Pz.Kpfw.III crew was increased up to 5 people and allowed the tank commander to concentrate on his core duties. The battles in France clearly showed the weakness of the 37 mm Kw.K.35/36 L/46.5 gun, which was a tank version of the Pak 35/36 anti-tank cannon. The Pz.Kpfw.III concept envisaged the development of a combat vehicle primarily to fight against enemy tanks, but the French B1-bis tanks appeared to be too tough for it. The installation of a more powerful 50 Kw.K 38 L/42 gun resolved this problem to some extent, but the limits were again shown after the beginning of Operation Barbarossa when the Pz.Kpfw.III encountered the Soviet T-34 and KV-1 tanks.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_IIIF_Spat]
Order for development of medium tank was issued in 1934. It should have had mass up to 15 tons and had one 37mm gun and three 7.92 machineguns. This project was called Zugfuhrerwagen – company commander vehicle – because of secrecy: in fact this vehicle was developed as main battle tank for Reichswehr and later Wehrmacht, each tank battalion should have had three companies of these “company commander vehicles”.
Daimler-Benz design was selected as the best one out of designs developed by four companies, it was called Panzerkampfwagen III (Sd.Kfz.141). First 10, experimental tanks Pz.Kpfw.III Ausf.A were made in 1936, they had suspension with 5 rollers for each side, each roller had separate spring suspension. Next two series, Ausf. B and Ausf.C (12 and 15 tanks made) were equipped with new suspension system with 8 rollers at each side with leaf springs.
Next series, Ausf. D (30 tanks) still had complex and expensive leaf-spring suspension, but it was more fitting for battle conditions nonetheless. Armor thickness was increased and tank mass increased too to 19.8 tons.
First mass produced variant of Pz. III was Ausf. E (96 tanks), these tanks were equipped with torsion suspension (developed by Ferdinand Porsche). New tanks also had new engine, new double-winged hatches on turret sides, ball and socket joint for machinegun was changed.
Production of new series, Pz.Kpfw.III Ausf.F (435 produced), was started at September 1939, these tanks had air intakes for brakes cooling installed of front hull. Last 100 tanks were armed with 50mm Kw.K 38 L/42 tank gun installed in new gun mantle (one of the coaxial machine guns was removed). All tanks of series E and F were rearmed in the same way later.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_BfWgIIIAusfK_Kursk1943]
Panzerbefhlswagen Ausf.K Medium Command Tank

The command tank Pz. Bef.Wg III Ausf K was serially produced from December 1942 to February 1943. It was designed for in-field control of tank operations. This modification was the pinnacle of command tanks based on Pz.III development, which had begun back in 1938.

Technically it was built upon the Pz.III Ausf L chassis. The turret was taken from the Pz.IV Ausf F, and a 50mm KwK L/60 gun was mounted in the turret with a specially designed mantlet, which had an observation gear as well. The tank had no machine guns, and instead there were pistol ports. The front plate of the topside was enhanced by a bolted 20mm armoured plate; later modifications had additional side armour skirts. Smoke dischargers were installed on the turret. The tank was equipped with a powerful FuG 8 radio set with a separate antenna in the rear end of the hull.

The tank was highly praised for combining decent weaponry and armour and with comfort for the commander and radio operator. A total of 50 such tanks were produced. They took an active part in battles, mainly on the Eastern Front. As of July 4, 1943 there were 8 command tanks in the Panzergrenadier-Division Grossdeutschland.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_IIIJ_L42_Kursk1943]
Panzerkampfwagen III Ausf.J mit 5cm KwK L/42 Medium Tank with a 50mm KwK L/42 gun

The final Pz III variant to be armed with a short 50mm KwK L/42 gun entered serial production in March of 1941. The new version had improved frontal armour, 50mm thick, and a thicker gun mantlet as well. The hull was also slightly redesigned, with a new driver’s visor and a ball mount for the machine gun. 

Tanks of this type were to be the most widely produced of all the Pz III models. By the time serial production ended in December of 1942, the factories at MAN, Daimler-Benz, Henschel, Alkett, Wegmann, MNH and MIAG had produced a total of 1,549 Pz III Ausf.J with KwK L/42. This unit saw the bulk of its action on the Eastern Front in 1941 and 1942, during which time it proved itself quite capable in combat. Its high-quality homogenous steel armour was effective against most Allied 40mm and 45mm anti-tank weapons. Considering the fact that 76mm AP projectiles were in short supply in 1941 and early 1942, it was not uncommon for Allied troops to fire HE and shrapnel projectiles at these tanks, although the Ausf.J proved itself adequately protected against these munitions as well. The tank’s short gun was quite accurate and effective against machine-gun nests and mortar and artillery positions, and most Soviet light tanks, such as the T-26 and BT, could also be easily knocked out with its AP projectiles. However, the armour of the Soviet T-34’s presented a problem, and the KV heavy tank could not be penetrated at all. The eventual availability of APCR rounds (Pzgr.40 with tungsten core, and Pzgr.40/1 with steel core) made the problem less noticeable, but nevertheless, by late 1941 this model was clearly obsolete. Subsequently, an up-gunned Pz.III Ausf.J with a longer 50mm KwK L/60 gun went into production, and some of the remaining short-gun variants were upgraded in the field. 

Several tanks of this type saw service in North Africa. Initially they performed quite well, for their frontal armour was able to withstand hits from British 2-pdr guns, while their main guns could knock out most Allied light tanks. But with the appearance of the Matilda and Valentine tanks, followed by the 6-pdr anti-tank guns and US M3 and M4 Medium tanks in 1942, the situation quickly turned: the tank was hopelessly outgunned. Nevertheless, a few Pz.III Ausf Js remained in active service until the German surrender in Tunisia.

Even though the Pz.III with KwK L/42 was deemed obsolete by 1943, they could still be found in some regiments. For instance, the Division Grossdeutschland had 2 battle-ready Ausf.Js on July 4th 1943.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_IIIJ]
Panzerkampfwagen III Ausf.J mit 5cm KwK 39 L/60 with 50mm KwK 39 L/60 gun

During the Battle of France, German tank crews encountered rather well-armored British and French tanks, including the S35, B1bis, and Matilda I and II. Engagements showed that the German 37mm tank guns were ineffective against such opposition. As a result, by August of 1940, the German High Command ordered the development of new Pz.III variants with a longer 50mm gun, which had much better armor penetration capability. However, satisfying the requirement took some time. Integrating a longer gun into existing turrets turned out to be a difficult task, while a short-barrel 50mm gun could go in easily. As an interim solution, the Pz.III Ausf.G, Ausf.H and Ausf.J received the short main gun.

Encounters with Soviet T-34 and KV tanks, and British Matilda and Valentine tanks in the summer of 1941, made the task of refitting Pz.IIIs with 50mm guns even more crucial. New Panzerkampfwagen III Ausf.J mit 5cm KwK 39 L/60 began to roll off the assembly lines in December of 1941. Some existing Pz.IIIs were also upgraded. Initial engagements in early 1942 proved promising; however it quickly became apparent that while the gun was adequate, the tank’s armor was not. The Russians overcame initial logistic difficulties and by then had 76mm AP shells in ample supply, while the Allies in North Africa began using 6-pdr anti-tank guns, and M3 and M4 Medium tanks with 75mm guns. By July of 1942, after a production run of 1,067 tanks, production of the Pz.III Ausf.J was stopped. They were replaced with the better armored Pz.III Ausf.L. Nevertheless, many Ausf.Js remained in active service throughout 1943.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_IIIL]
Panzerkampfwagen III Ausf.L Medium Tank

The Pz.III Ausf L. tank, which was serially produced from June 1942, was a further development of the Pz.III Ausf J. modification. The main changes in design were aimed at strengthening the tank's armour. The thickness of the turret's front plate was increased from 30mm to 57mm, and the front part of the topside and the gun's mantlet were furthered armoured with an additional 20mm thick plate, which was mounted in front of the main armour with a 120mm gap. This proved quite an effective armour design, as the first plate caused the detonator of an armour-piercing projectile to explode in the gap, which meant that the main plate escaped from harm. There were several other changes worth mentioning. For example, the gun balance unit was changed from spring-based suspension to a torsion mechanism, the design of the air inlets was changed, the ammo loadout was slightly reduced, the loader’s observation device was removed, and the system for hot coolant transfer from one tank to another was implemented (which was very timely for vehicles used on the Eastern Front in winter). Since the spring of 1943 some of these tanks were additionally equipped with mounted armour panels.

At first, MAN, Daimler-Benz, Henschel, Alkett, Wegmann, MNH, and MIAG received an order for 1,100 tanks of that type, but in the end some of the chassis was reallotted for the Pz.III Ausf N production. Thus, a total of 653 vehicles had left the assembly line by the termination of the model's production in December 1942. As of 1942, tanks of this type were regarded as quite up-to-date - a 'long' 50mm KwK L/60 gun could destroy a T-34, and could make even quicker work of the very common T-60 and T-70. There were plenty of APCR projectiles for the gun, and with their help it could penetrate KV armour as well, if the unlucky target came within the range of 500m. The armour of the tank, especially in the front, gave it decent protection from the most widespread Soviet 45mm antitank and tank guns. Excellent observation devices and the presence of a dedicated commander enabled it to fight Soviet tanks on equal terms. Nevertheless, the gradual outdating of the vehicle soon became evident, and Pz.III Ausf M that had replaced Pz.III Ausf L on the assembly line was the last classic Pz.III.

Pz.III Ausf L tanks were used in many parts of the world. They saw action in Africa, Italy, and France. But most of these tanks were used on the Eastern Front, especially in battles at the end of 1942 and in early 1943. In the Battle of Kursk, tanks of this modification were the most numerous of all the Pz.III tanks. Together with the Pz.IV Ausf G, they served as the backbone of the German tank forces. As of July 4, 1943 there were twenty of Pz.III tanks with a ’long’ 50mm gun in Panzergrenadier-Division Grossdeutschland; by July 31, six of these tanks were decommissioned as irrecoverable losses.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_IIIN]
Panzerkampfwagen III Ausf.N Medium Support Tank, 1942

The Pz.III Ausf.N tank entered serial production in June of 1942. This was a fire support tank, filling a niche vacated when production of short-barreled Pz.IVs and StuG.IIIs ended. Despite the 75mm gun’s poor armour penetration capabilities, it nevertheless had its advantages. Its low muzzle velocity led to improved accuracy, which made it well suited for attacking fixed emplacements such as machine-gun nests and artillery and mortar positions. Stock Pz.IIIs armed with 50mm guns were quickly becoming obsolete, and it was determined that some of them would be used to create a specialized fire support tank, which was designated Pz.III Ausf.N. A large number of these Ausf.Ns were made by converting incomplete Ausf.L and Ausf.M Pz.IIIs on the assembly line.

The tank’s main weapon was a 75mm StuK L/24 gun, of which there were a surplus after the StuG III SPG was upgraded to a better weapon. The Ausf.N had split frontal armour, with an additional 20mm armour plate installed 120mm ahead of the main armour plate. Some vehicles had additional armour on the gun mantlet, and the late-series models had armour skirts and a new commander’s cupola with thicker armour and a single hatch. Some of these tanks were covered in Zimmerit, an anti-magnetic paste. The Ausf.N ended up being the final production variant of the Panzer III. When production ended in August of 1943, seven hundred Ausf.Ns had been produced, including three converted Ausf.Js, 447 converted Ausf.Ls and 213 converted Ausf.Ms; thirty-seven additional examples were made from other Pz.III models, converted when they returned to the factory for repairs. Altogether, the Pz.III Ausf.N was a successful tank for 1943, despite its lack of heavy armour.

The Pz.III Ausf.N was actively used on all fronts until the end of the war, though very few still remained in service in 1945. It saw action in Africa, Italy, France, and Eastern Europe. Some saw action in panzergrenadier divisions, where they sat alongside heavy Tiger tanks in special battalions.

In North Africa, the Pz.III Ausf.N became famous for its inclusion in the 501st heavy tank battalion, which, for example, had sixteen Tigers and fourteen Pz.III Ausf.Ns in February of 1943. With the 501st, the Ausf.N participated in heavy fighting in Tunisia starting in November of 1942, and continued there until the end of the North African campaign.

The Division Grossdeutschland had only two battle-ready Ausf.Ns on July 4th 1943, though there were a total sixty such tanks on the southern flank of Kursk salient. 

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_IIIN_afrika]
Panzerkampfwagen III Ausf.N Medium Support Tank, 1942

The Pz.III Ausf.N tank entered serial production in June of 1942. This was a fire support tank, filling a niche vacated when production of short-barreled Pz.IVs and StuG.IIIs ended. Despite the 75mm gun’s poor armour penetration capabilities, it nevertheless had its advantages. Its low muzzle velocity led to improved accuracy, which made it well suited for attacking fixed emplacements such as machine-gun nests and artillery and mortar positions. Stock Pz.IIIs armed with 50mm guns were quickly becoming obsolete, and it was determined that some of them would be used to create a specialized fire support tank, which was designated Pz.III Ausf.N. A large number of these Ausf.Ns were made by converting incomplete Ausf.L and Ausf.M Pz.IIIs on the assembly line.

The tank’s main weapon was a 75mm StuK L/24 gun, of which there were a surplus after the StuG III SPG was upgraded to a better weapon. The Ausf.N had split frontal armour, with an additional 20mm armour plate installed 120mm ahead of the main armour plate. Some vehicles had additional armour on the gun mantlet, and the late-series models had armour skirts and a new commander’s cupola with thicker armour and a single hatch. Some of these tanks were covered in Zimmerit, an anti-magnetic paste. The Ausf.N ended up being the final production variant of the Panzer III. When production ended in August of 1943, seven hundred Ausf.Ns had been produced, including three converted Ausf.Js, 447 converted Ausf.Ls and 213 converted Ausf.Ms; thirty-seven additional examples were made from other Pz.III models, converted when they returned to the factory for repairs. Altogether, the Pz.III Ausf.N was a successful tank for 1943, despite its lack of heavy armour.

The Pz.III Ausf.N was actively used on all fronts until the end of the war, though very few still remained in service in 1945. It saw action in Africa, Italy, France, and Eastern Europe. Some saw action in panzergrenadier divisions, where they sat alongside heavy Tiger tanks in special battalions.

In North Africa, the Pz.III Ausf.N became famous for its inclusion in the 501st heavy tank battalion, which, for example, had sixteen Tigers and fourteen Pz.III Ausf.Ns in February of 1943. With the 501st, the Ausf.N participated in heavy fighting in Tunisia starting in November of 1942, and continued there until the end of the North African campaign.

The Division Grossdeutschland had only two battle-ready Ausf.Ns on July 4th 1943, though there were a total sixty such tanks on the southern flank of Kursk salient. 

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_IIIM]
PzKpfw III Ausf. M medium tank

In October of 1942, production began on the Pz.Kpfw.III Ausf.M -- the final modification of the Pz.Kpfw.III series. The tank was the final development of the Pz.Kpfw.III Ausf.L, and, like its predecessor, was equipped with additional spaced armour, located along the front of the hull and the gun mantlet. The major difference between the new unit and the Pz.Kpfw.III Ausf.M was a new muffler which increased its maximum fording depth to 1.3 meters. Also, the escape hatches on the hull sides were removed, and smoke grenade dischargers were mounted on the turret sides, while the spotlights changed position slightly. Since 1943, these tanks were equipped with side skirts, which decreased the effect of hollow-charge rounds. 

Only 250 of the scheduled 1,000 tanks were produced according to the original design. Another 100 Pz.Kpfw.II were built as Flammpanzer III (Pz.Kpfw.III (Fl)) flame-thrower tanks, and yet another 213 vehicles came off the production line as Pz.Kpfw.III Ausf.N close support tanks. Another 165 hulls were repurposed for the production of StuG.40 Ausf.G assault guns.

By the time the Pz.Kpfw.III Ausf.M entered production, it had already ceased to be the primary tank of the Wehrmacht. It could still stand against the T-34 and KV-1, but at distances of only up to 500m. By the time the tank found its way to the field, the troops were being supplied with the Pz.Kpfw.IV Ausf.G tank, which boasted the far more powerful 75 mm Kw.K.L/48 gun.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/pz_ivc]
PzKpfw IV Ausf.C medium tank

In 1934, the Army Ordnance Department ordered the development of a medium tank designated as Zugfuhrerwagen (ZW), i.e. a platoon commander’s vehicle. This tank became known later under the designation Pz.Kpfw.III. Simultaneously, an order was issued to develop a support tank armed with a short-barrel 75 mm gun and weighing no more than 24 tons. This tank was designated as Bataillonsfuhrerwagen (BW), i.e. battalion commander’s vehicle. Just as with theZugfuhrerwagen, this was a deliberately misleading name: according to the organizational chart each tank battalion was to include one platoon of these tanks. The BW was a medium tank on paper, but it was classified as heavy tankby the Germans.
Three firms took part in the contest for the design of the Bataillonsfuhrerwagen, and finally Krupp’s design VK2001/K was chosen. Interesting enough is that this design was a redesigned version of the MKA tank which had participated in the contest for the development of the Zugfuhrerwagen. Krupp Co. had to considerably redesign the BW, in particular rebuild the running gear which finally consisted of eight dual road wheels per side. The road wheels were grouped into bogies suspended on leaf springs. Unlike the Pz.Kpfw.III, the road wheel suspension of the new tank proved successful and remained almost unchanged throughout its production period. 
The first BW prototypes were ready in 1936. By that time the vehicle was designated Panzerkampfwagen IV and received the official Wehrmacht designation Sd.Kfz.161. The Pz.Kpfw.IV Ausf.A was only a little heavier than the Pz.Kpfw.III Ausf.A. The first Pz.Kpfw.IV had quite modest armor (the maximum armor thickness being 20 mm) and only a total of 35 Pz.Kpfw.IV Ausf.A tanks were produced. In 1937, the production of an improved model, the Pz.Kpfw.IV Ausf.B, began. The tank was up-armored, yet for some unclear reasons the ball mount of the machine gun was removed (restored again on the Pz.Kpfw.IV Ausf.D). The front armor plate was straightened and a number of tanks received an improved commander’s cupola. A total of 45 Pz.Kpfw.IV Ausf.B were produced.
Since 1938, the first mass produced version of the Pz.Kpfw.IV entered production known as Pz.Kpfw.IV Ausf.C. It was a substantiallyimproved version of the Pz.Kpfw.IV Ausf.B. A new engine was installed in the tank. A total of 134 tanks of this model were produced.
By the beginning of the Polish Campaign the Pz.Kpfw.IV tanks outnumbered the Pz.Kpfw.III tanks, which they were supposed to support. The first Pz.Kpw.IV models had quite weak armor and their gun was better capable to destroy enemy infantry rather than tanks. The 75 mm Kw.K.37 L/24 gun was efficient against the lightly armored 7TP and English cruiser tanks, but it could inflict little damage on such vehicles as the Somua S-35 not to mention the B1 bis and the Matilda II. Fighting against tanks, however, was not the major task of the Pz.Kpfw.IV. In terms of design it was a more successful vehicle than the Pz.Kpfw.III, with large room for improvement.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_IVF1]
Panzerkampfwagen IV Ausf.F1 Medium Support Tank

The last Pz IV variant, armed with a short-barrel 75mm KwK L/24 gun, entered production in April of 1941. This new variant had improved 50mm armour, which protected the frontal parts of the hull and turret, as well as 30mm side armour. The tank’s weight grew to 22.3 tons, which required widening its tracks from 380 to 400mm, with consequent changes to all its wheels.

The tank proved itself quite capable during combat in Russia and North Africa. Its forward steel armour was effective against most Allied 40mm and 45mm anti-tank weapons. Considering the fact that 76mm AP projectiles were in short supply in 1941 and early 1942, it was not uncommon for Allied troops to fire HE and shrapnel projectiles at these tanks, although the Ausf.F1 proved itself adequately protected against these munitions as well. The tank’s short gun was quite accurate and effective against the most common targets that a fire support tank usually has to face, such as machine-gun and artillery positions. Most Soviet light tanks, such as the T-26 and BT, could be easily knocked out with its AP projectiles. When engaging the heavier Soviet T-34 and KV tanks, HEAT projectile were used instead. Nevertheless, by early 1942, the weapon’s effectiveness was becoming less than desirable, which led to the up-gunned Ausf.F2 variant, which was essentially an F1 with a 75mm KwK 40 L/43 gun. The combined forces of Krupp, Vomag, and Nibelungenwerk  produced a total of 462 Pz.IV Ausf.F1 by March of 1942 when production ended; twenty-five of them were immediately converted into Pz.IV Ausf.F2.

This model saw most of its field combat on the Eastern Front between the autumn of 1941 and summer of 1942, battles in which a great many of these units were lost. Many of these tanks were also used in North Africa, especially in 1941 and early 1942. By late 1943, some short-gun Pz.IV still remained in service, for example the Division Grossdeutschland  had seven battle-ready Ausf.F1s on July 4th 1943, even though the model was by then hopelessly obsolete. The new Soviet 45mm M-42 anti-tank guns could easily penetrate its armour, and 76mm AP rounds were also widely available. In North Africa, it encountered dire threats even earlier, with the appearance of the British 6-pdr (57mm) anti-tank gun, and the US M3 and M4 Sherman tanks. However, individual examples of the Ausf.F1 continued to see sporadic action until the end of the war.


[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_IVF2]
Panzerkampfwagen IV Ausf.F2 Medium Tank

By the summer of 1941, it was becoming clear that the Pz.IV armed with a short 75mm KwK L/24 gun was ineffective against Soviet T-34 and KV tanks. Use of HEAT rounds made the problem somewhat manageable, but it was evident that a better armed tank was needed, capable of penetrating thicker armour. A gun fit for the task was the 75mm KwK 40 L/43. By March of 1942, Pz.IV Ausf.F2 began to roll off assembly lines, which were basically up-gunned Pz.IV Ausf.F1s. Other than the new main gun, there were practically no differences between them.

Pz.IV Ausf.F2 was manufactured by Krupp, Vomag, and Nibelungenwerk, with a very short production run. After producing only 175 F2s (with 25 more F1s converted to the new gun), the production ended in July of 1942. The manufacturers switched over to the new Pz.IV Ausf.G variant, which initially had only minor differences from its predecessors. 

The bulk of the action for this model took place on the Eastern Front in the summer and fall of 1942, where it immediately became popular with the crews due to the effectiveness of its main gun. With its introduction, Soviet T-34 and KV tanks virtually lost all their advantages, and the Germans were finally on par with the Russians in terms of tank armour and armament. The model was also used in North Africa, where its appearance was an unpleasant surprise to the Allies, whose tanks were now being knocked out at long range. However, by 1943, the variant all but lost its superiority. Its armour was not very effective against the British 6-pdr anti-tank gun, and 75mm guns of the US M3 and M4 Sherman tanks, which were generally on equal terms with the Ausf.F2. Nevertheless, the variant continued to see action in Tunisia and Libya until early 1943. By the time of the Battle of Kursk, few tanks of this type remained in active service. Since the improved armament was not accompanied by improved armour, the tank’s frontal armour could not hope to stop anything beyond a 45mm projectile, which led to heavy losses. Nevertheless, some individual examples of the Ausf.F2 continued to see sporadic action until the end of the war.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_IVG]
Panzerkampfwagen IV Ausf.G Medium Tank

The Pz.IV Ausf G modification, which was under production since May 1942, was a natural development of the Pz.IV Ausf F2. At first there were only minor differences, but in the course of the mass production, which terminated in 1943, the vehicle underwent several changes, and as a result the tanks of the last production batches were far closer to the next model, the Pz.IV Ausf H. Of principle importance was the enhanced armour. From the end of June 1942 an additional 30mm armour plate was mounted on some of the tanks. It was mounted on the front armour of the hull and the top; moreover, there were two variations in the manner of the plate's attachment, which was done with either welding or bolting. Other gradual changes included a new muzzle brake, a system for the transfer of heated coolant from one vehicle to another (very timely for vehicles used on the Eastern Front in winter), and smoke grenade launchers at the sides of the turret instead of a smoke generator system. From January 1943 the driver's periscope was removed, and 5mm armour panels were installed on the tanks, which greatly increased the tank's protection against the impacts of AT rifles, small-calibre AP and composite projectiles. Beginning in March of 1943, the tank received a longer 75mm KwK 40 L/43 gun, and the manhole of the commander's pod was changed to a one-leaf design.

A total of 1,687 Pz.IV Ausf G tanks were produced by the Krupp, Vomag and Nibelungenwerke Companies. Tanks of this modification, along with the Pz.III Ausf L tanks, were the backbone of German tank forces in the Battle of Kursk. Thus, as of July 4, 1943 there were 55 Pz.IV tanks with long 75mm guns (Pz.IV Ausf G mainly) in the Panzergrenadier-Division Grossdeutschland. A total of sixteen Pz.IV tanks of all modifications of that division were lost during the Battle of Kursk. These Pz.IV Ausf G tanks were used until the end of the war, though their numbers dwindled.

In the summer of 1943, the Pz.IV Ausf G with enhanced armour and side armour skirts was a formidable threat to the T-34 and T-70 tanks, which served as the backbone of the Soviet tank forces. The front armour plate provided the tank reliable protection against 45mm and 76mm antitank and tank guns, and its 75mm KwK 40 gun could hit Soviet tanks at most common combat ranges. The weak spots of the tank were its side armour and the lack of protection covering the front turret.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_IVH]
Panzerkampfwagen IV Ausf.H medium tank

Mass production of the Pz.Kpfw.IV Ausf.H began in April 1943. A total of 3,374 tanks of this type were manufactured, the majority share of the Pz.Kpfw.IV family. Its armour was better reinforced than the previous models, with its front armour plate reaching a thickness of 80 mm. Its suspension was reinforced as well, which helped with the increased load on the front road wheels. Like the later Ausf.G model, the Ausf.H was equipped with 5-mm-thick skirts to protect against hollow-charge rounds. Zimmerit anti-magnetic paste was also applied to protect the tanks against magnetic mines. These tanks saw continuous re-designed during their production lifetime: all-steel return rollers were added (due to the shortage of rubber), and the road wheels and idlers were re-shaped. Production of the Pz.Kpfw.IV Ausf.H halted in July 1944, at which time it was replaced by a simplified model, the Pz.Kpfw.IV Ausf.J.

The front armour of the Pz.Kpfw.IV was reinforced, making it immune to Soviet 76 mm tank guns. On the other hand, most shells would hit the turret, where its 50-mm-thick armour remained unchanged. Its weight increased to 26 tons, making the tank became less manoeuvrable, which could be a serious drawback in a tank battle. Nevertheless, the Pz.Kpfw.IV Ausf.H was a dangerous presence on the battlefield, capable of damaging enemy tanks from outside of the range of their guns.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_VA_Panther]
Panzerkampfwagen V Ausf. A Panther

In 1938, development began on a new medium tank designed to replace the PzKpfw III and IV. The 20-ton prototype, designated the VK 20.01, was to be presented by Daimler-Benz, Krupp, and MAN. Development, however, was sluggish, because the military was already satisfied with the reliability and combat capability of their current line of medium tanks. The chassis was mostly completed by the fall of 1941, but by that time the situation had already changed.

In their engagements with the Soviet Union, German forces had encountered the new Soviet T-34 and KV tanks. In order to study these vehicles, there was established a special committee, which included designers Ferdinand Porsche and Ernst Kniepkamp. Upon analyzing the advantages and drawbacks of the T-34 and other Soviet tanks, the engineers decided that German tanks would undergo multiple improvements, including sloped armour, a large-roller chassis, and wider tracks. The development of the 20-ton tank was cancelled, and instead, on November 25, 1941, Daimler-Benz and MAN were entrusted with the production of a 25-ton prototype that would feature these newly-ordered improvements. This promising new tank was designated "Panther."

In the spring of 1942, both contractors introduced their prototypes. The Daimler-Benz model closely resembled the T-34, even sharing the same layout, with the engine-transmission compartment in the back. The MAN prototype was a purely German tank, featuring the transmission compartment in the front and the engine compartment in the back, as well as Kniepkamp’s torsion-bar suspension. As for the primary calibre, the tank boasted a 75mm long-barreled gun made by Rheinmetall, specially selected by the Fuehrer. The relatively low calibre was chosen to increase the tank’s rate of fire and its ammunition capacity. After a series of comparative tests, the MAN prototype was deemed preferable, as it had a better suspension, higher mobility, shorter barrel overhang, and so on. It entered mass production in January 1943. The pre-series Ausf. A (Ausf. D1) was followed by high-volume production of the Ausf. D (Ausf. D2) model.

As early as February 1943, it was determined that the Ausf. А required improvements. The new model would have a significantly redesigned turret, which featured a new commander’s cupola, a different gun mantlet, and a new turret rotation mechanism. Then, in late 1943, the binocular sight was replaced with a monocular, and the tank finally received a decent machine gun socket mount.

Later, the Panther Ausf. А tank, 2000 of which were produced from August 1943 to May 1944, was replaced by the more manufacturable Ausf. G. model. Nevertheless, it was the Panther Ausf. A that became famous for the great troubles it caused for the Allied tankmen in Normandy.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_VD_Panther_Kursk1943]
Panzerkampfwagen V Ausf.D "Panther" Medium Tank

In 1938, German designers began work on a new medium tank to replace the Pz.Kpfw. III and Pz.Kpfw. IV. The project of this 20 ton vehicle, which was being developed by Daimler-Benz, Krupp and MAN Companies, was designated the VK 20.01. Work on the new tank proceeded rather slowly, because their reliable and battle-tested medium tanks already well suited the needs of the German military. Nevertheless, by the autumn of 1941 the chassis design was effectively completed, though by that time the status of the war had changed.

During the war with the Soviet Union, German forces had to deal with the new Soviet T-34 and KV tanks. For the study of Soviet tanks, the German military and technical experts created a special committee, which included key German armoured vehicle designers, and F. Porsche and E. Kniepkamp in particular. These German designers thoroughly studied all the advantages and disadvantages of the T-34 and other Soviet tanks, and decided to introduce such features in upcoming German tank designs as sloping armour plating, big-wheeled chassis and wide tracks. Work on a 20 ton tank was halted, and on November 25, 1941, Daimler-Benz and the MAN Companies secured an order for designing a 35 ton tank incorporating all the above-mentioned design concepts. The new tank project received the code name Panther.

In the spring of 1942, both contractors presented their prototypes. The test vehicle of Daimler-Benz was very much alike T-34 tank in appearance, for German designers borrowed its rear-placed engine transmission compartment. The prototype of MAN AG was designed along the lines of German tank-building tradition, with a front transmission and rear engine compartments, and individual torsion suspension with a staggered arrangement of tracks designed by G. Kniepkamp. The 75mm long-barrel Rheinmetall gun, which was chosen by Führer, was installed on the tank as its main weapon. A gun of relatively small calibre was chosen in order to get a higher rate of fire and to increase the reserve ammunition that could be carried inside the tank. Comparative testing showed the advantages of MAN AG's project (better suspension, manoeuvrability, lesser barrel recoil, etc.), and thus it was accepted for serial production, which started in January of 1943. After some pre-production, Ausf A vehicles were under manufacture, and large-series production of Ausf D tanks began.

The first units to get the new tanks were the 51st and 52nd tank battalions. Each received 96 Panthers, along with other vehicles, in May 1943; a month later both battalions joined the 39th tank regiment. There were 200 vehicles in the regiment: 96 in each battalion and 8 more tanks in regiment headquarters. Major Lauchert was appointed as the 39th tank regiment commander. Before the launch of Operation Citadel, the 10th Tank Brigade was formed, which included the 39th tank regiment and the tank regiment of the Panzergrenadier-Division Grossdeutschland. Colonel Dekker was appointed to brigade commander. The brigade was under the operational control of the Panzergrenadier-Division Grossdeutschland.

The tanks were delivered to the staging area from the 1st through the 3rd of July, several days before the operation began. Panthers saw combat action for the first time in history on July 5th, 1943, in an assault on the Soviet forces near Cherkasskoe village. During the first several days of the operation, the roster of battle-ready tanks drastically decreased; thus, by the evening of July 7th, there were only forty of such tanks, and by the evening of July 10th, only ten remained. However, irrecoverable losses were not so heavy; by the evening of July 10th, only twenty-five tanks were decommissioned. Most of the Panthers required only a short-term repair; besides, many of these tanks broke down due to technical glitches. These were new, untested tanks, unfamiliar to their crews, and they were called upon to break through well-prepared defensive positions which were full of mines and bravely defended by Soviet troops. By July 20th, fifty-six tanks were counted among the irrecoverable losses, and sixteen were sent back to Germany for extensive repairs, while eighty-five tanks were being repaired on site. The Germans estimated that 269 Soviet tanks were lost in engagement with the Panthers.

Summarizing the results of the battle, both German and Soviet experts closely studied the chief advantages of the new tank. They featured a powerful gun and heavy front armour, which provided effective protection from Soviet antitank and tank artillery, and in some cases from powerful AA and corps guns. At the same time, the armouring of tank sides and rear end was much lighter, and could be penetrated even with 45mm antitank and tank guns.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/pz_vg_(panther)]
Panzerkampfwagen V Ausf. G Panther

In May of 1943, German military command ordered a new Panther modification, which would have a simpler design for easier manufacture. The new Ausf. G differed primarily from the Ausf. А by its hull design. The new model did away with the sloped floor of the engine compartment, which entailed raising its radiators, which, in turn, entailed decreasing the slope angle of its side plates from 40 to 29 degrees. Its plate thickness was also increased to 50mm. Improvements to the power unit resulted in fewer exhaust pipes, and the ammunition capacity of the tank was increased to 82 shells. Finally, the number of side skirts was reduced from six to five per side, although these tanks often fought without side skirts at all.

The Ausf. G became the last and the most common Panther modification. The Ausf. F, as well as the new Panther II, would never enter mass production. From its launch in March 1944, production of the Ausf. G continued through the end of the war, over which time a total of 3126 vehicles were produced.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_VIH1_Tiger]
Panzerkampfwagen VI Ausf. H1 "Tiger" Heavy Tank

The works on design of a new heavy tank began in Germany already in 1937. Initially the new tank was envisioned as a 30 ton vehicle, protected with 50mm armour and equipped with a short-barrel 75mm gun. Initially the work was carried out by the Henschel AG design bureau, whose first prototypes were tested in 1938 through 1941 (the DW1, DW2 and VK 3001(H)). These had little in common with the future Tiger, and neither did the experimental model VK 3001(P) (developed by Porsche), which joined the project in 1939.

In May of 1941 Hitler defined a new concept for the heavy tank: the Führer believed that it should be a combat vehicle equipped with a gun that could penetrate armour of any of current or future tank, and protected with heavy armour. On May 26, 1941 Henschel and Porsche were ordered to create such a tank. Initially, Henschel AG was supposed to provide the tank with a 75mm gun with a coned bore, which provided very high armour penetration.

Each company based their designs on previously tested prototypes, and significantly remodelled their design for enhancing armour protection and weaponry. The coned bore gun was soon abandoned due to the acute shortage of tungsten, which was necessary for the production of its projectiles. As the result of this, the first prototypes of the new tank, the VK 3601(H), were left unarmed, and Henschel had to urgently redesign its tank to fit it with an 88mm conventional gun, which had been designed for Porsche's tank. In April of 1942, the competing companies presented to the Führer their prototypes of the new tank, the VK 4501(H) and VK 4501(P). Despite similar combat characteristics, the vehicles differed fundamentally in design: the Porsche tank had an original propulsion system and transmission, comprised of two air-cooled engines, two electric generators and two electro motors. The chassis of the prototype differed significantly as well.

The initial plan was to begin production of the tanks of both companies. It was evident that the Führer favoured Porsche's vehicle, and it was put into serial production before the results of the official test were even announced. But the tests carried out in May 1942 revealed the Henschel tank was more reliable and possessed significantly better manoeuvrability and endurance. As a result, that tank was finally accepted, and the ninety Porsche tanks under production were converted into Ferdinand antitank SP guns (though a single Porsche tank saw action in 1944).

Serial production of Tigers began in August 1941, and initially progressed slowly; thus, in 1942 only 72 tanks had been manufactured. Nevertheless, by the end of August, four tanks were dispatched to the area of Leningrad.
The Tiger was a rather well-balanced tank. In 1943, when it entered actual combat operations, its gun easily penetrated all Soviet and Allied tanks, with the single exception of the front armour of the English heavy tank Churchill. At the same time, its heavy armour provided effective protection from the most common antitank and tank guns of the enemy. The Tiger's armour could not be penetrated with armour-piercing projectiles of 45mm antitank and tank guns, but only with composite projectiles, from the side or from behind, and from no greater distance than 200-350m. Armour-piercing projectiles of 76mm guns (BR-350B, BR-350SP) could penetrate only the side armour of the tank and only from a short distance. BR-350A projectiles and 76mm hollow charges could not penetrate Tiger's armour at all. The tank could also be penetrated from side or rear with projectiles of English 57mm and American 75mm tank guns. At the same time, from the front Tiger was invincible to custom AT weapons, and enemies had to bring powerful corps ordnances and AA guns to engage it.

Nevertheless, the Tiger had some drawbacks: the vehicle was difficult to operate and it required constant maintenance. The huge weight of the tank limited its manoeuvrability and made it difficult to evacuate damaged vehicles.

152 Tigers were used in Operation Citadel (there were 133 vehicles as of the beginning of the operation, and 19 more were dispatched as reinforcements). They comprised the two heavy tank battalions (the 503rd and 505th) and four separate tank companies under command of Grossdeutschland, the Leibstandarte Adolf Hitler, the Totenkopf, and Das Reich Waffen-SS divisions. The 9th tank regiment of the Panzergrenadier-Division Grossdeutschland was formed on January 13, 1943, and in February through March, the company took an active role in German counteroffensive near Kharkov. By the beginning of Operation Citadel, there were fourteen intact Tigers in the company. The company had suffered no irrecoverable losses in battles, though tanks were consistently removed from action due Soviet firing and technical failures. In these cases, maintenance companies quickly recovered the damaged vehicles.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_VIE_Tiger]
Panzerkampfwagen VI Ausf. E Tiger Heavy Tank

Over the course of its production, the Tiger underwent several modifications informed by its battlefield performance. The most noticeable adjustment was a new, more comfortable commander’s cupola which resembled that of the Panther. Other changes included adding a loader’s vision device, removing smoke grenades from tank’s ammunition (due to their low efficiency), varying its detachable equipment, and the introduction of the Zimmerit coating (from the fall of 1943 onwards).

Even though there was only a small number of Tigers in Normandy, they proved to be deadly in combat. The Allies had nothing that could match them. The Churchill tank, the closest in terms of armour, was hopelessly out-gunned, having a relatively low-power 75mm gun. The more common Shermans and Cromwells stood very little chance against a Tiger. (Except for a small number of Shermans equipped with the powerful 17-pounder guns.)

In spite of their enormous advantage, these few Tigers were not enough to turn the tides of war, and were eventually put out of service.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/pz_vib_(tiger_ii)]
Panzerkampfwagen VI Tiger II Ausf.B Heavy Tank

In May 1941, the decision was made to develop a promising new 35 to 45-ton tank. In August of the following year, the Wehrmacht’s Army Weapons Agency drafted a proposal for a new heavy tank to replace the PzKpfw VI Tiger I.

The draft was handed over to the design bureaus of Ferdinand Porsche and Erwin Aders. Two vehicles participated in the 1943 tests: the VK4502(P), made by Porsche, and VK4503(H), made by Aders. The latter prevailed, as it was simple to manufacture, while the Porsche tank had a rather complex and expensive electric transmission, similar to that of the Ferdinand SPG. However, Porsche built 50 cast towers to equip the first Tiger II Ausf. В units. The new vehicle entered mass production in November of 1943, and 3 prototypes were assembled by the end of the year. The tank remained in full production until the very end of the war, over which time a total of 489 units were produced.

The new tank was officially named the Pz.Kpfw VI Ausf. B (Sd.Kfz.182), often called the Tiger II or Tiger В. The unofficial name Königstiger (the German word for the Bengal tiger) was the popular name in post-war books. In Soviet literature it was often called the Tiger B or the King Tiger.

The layout of the Tiger II was typical of German tanks. The front part featured the driving compartment with the main clutch, the steering drive and gearbox, as well as some ammo loadout. The driver's seat was to the left of the gearbox, while the radio operator/bow gunner's seat was to the right. The fighting compartment was located in the middle of the tank, below the gun turret, and the gunner's station was to the left of the gun, in front of the commander. The gun-layer was seated to the right, and ammo was stowed in a niche by the turret and on the side shelves. The turret had a hydraulic-powered rotating floor, and the turret ring diameter was 1800mm. The engine compartment featured an engine, fans, and heatsinks, as well as fuel tanks. The fighting and engine compartments were divided by an armoured bulkhead.

Engine: Maybach HL210P30, later Maybach HL230P45. V12, carburetor, liquid-cooled. Engine power: HL210P30 - 478 kW (650 hp), HL230P45 - 515 kW (700 hp) at 3000 rpm. Fuel: ethylated gasoline. Gearbox: Maybach OLVAR OG(B) 40 12 16 A, hydraulically-controlled semi-automatic, pre-selector, 8 forward and 4 reverse gears.

Main armament: a 8.8 cm KwK43 L/71 gun based on the Flak 41 AA gun. Gun barrel length: 6298 mm (6595 mm with muzzle brake). Gun weight: 2265 kg. Elevation angles from -8 to +15 degrees. Ammo capacity: 84 rounds for Henschel turrets, 77 rounds for Porsche turrets.

The ammunition included AP calibre, sub-calibre, hollow-charge and HE shells.

A 7.92mm MG-34 machine gun (later replaced with MG-42) was twinned with the main gun. The frontal armour also boasted a ball-mounted machine gun, and an additional anti-aircraft machine gun could be installed in the commander's cupola.

The tank was heavily armoured, especially the frontal plate. The upper frontal plate was a full 150mm thick, and sloped at 50 degrees for improved survivability against most enemy tank and anti-tank guns. At the same time, the armour was too brittle, due to the acute shortage of alloying metals in Germany. For this reason, it could be easily destroyed by heavy HE and AP shells.

Tiger II tanks were adopted by heavy tank battalions (Schwere Panzer Panzerabteilungen), and replaced the Pz.Kpfw.VI (that is, the Tiger I). Such a battalion included 3 companies of 3 platoons of 14 units, including 2 commander tanks. A battalion totalled 45 tanks, including 3 command vehicles.

The 503rd battalion was the first to receive the new tanks. In July 1944, it fought against the Allied forces in Colombelles, France. In that battle, the new Tigers destroyed a few Shermans of the 148th Regiment of the Royal Armoured Corps.

On the Eastern Front, Tiger II tanks saw their first and quite unsuccessful combat action in August of 1944, where they were tasked with the elimination of the Sandomir bridgehead. In the course of that fateful battle, somewhere between 11 to 24 vehicles were lost, three of which were still-functional tanks captured by the Soviets. Remarkably, the Soviet 6th Tank Corps suffered no casualties or materiel damage during the battle against these marvels of modern German warfare. However, this was due more to excellent tactical capability of Soviet tank crews, than to the Tiger II's inherent technical drawbacks. Later, Tiger II tanks were actively used in the final phase of the World War II, in particular, during the last major offensive of the Germans in the Lake Balaton region, in Hungary.

The Tiger II was an ambiguous tank. It had obvious advantages (good armour, and a gun of excellent ballistic qualities), but it also had a number of drawbacks, including its very heavy weight and large dimensions, and its poor transmission for a vehicle of such weight, not to mention its high production cost. Nevertheless, the Tiger II was very dangerous when handled by an experienced crew. It is rightfully considered one of the most powerful tanks of World War II.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/ger/Pz_VIB_(Tiger_II_Porsche)]
Panzerkampfwagen VI Tiger Ausf. B with the Porsche Turret

In the final months of 1942, development began on a new heavy tank equipped with an 88mm long-barrelled gun. As with the Tiger’s development, two companies – Henschel and the design bureau of Ferdinand Porsche – were entrusted with the design. Porsche’s prototype was rejected once again, on account of problems with electric transmission, high manufacturing costs, and low manufacturability. Nevertheless, the effort was not entirely in vain, and the turret he designed was later used in a competing project.

The new vehicle was actively developed throughout 1943, and three pre-series prototypes were produced. Mass production was finally launched in January of 1944, but due to Allied air raids, only 20 vehicles had been assembled by March 1944. Beginning with the 51st unit off the assembly line, these tanks acquired a better turret, designed by Krupp, which had a flattened frontal armour plate.

Essentially, the King Tiger was a larger and a better-armoured version of the Panther. It was equipped with the powerful 88mm gun, but featured its predecessor’s engine. This power unit, along with its running gear, was fated to be the Achilles’ heel of the vehicle ¬– being overloaded, it was neither mobile nor reliable. Nevertheless, the powerful gun and thick armour made the King Tiger a deadly presence on the battlefield.

The 503rd heavy tank battalion was one of the first to obtain these new tanks. In June 1944, its 1st company boasted 12 King Tigers equipped with the Porsche turret. Redeployed to Normandy, the 503rd battalion actively fought against the British. In particular, on July 18, 1944, the 503rd, along with other armoured and infantry forces, engaged the British 7th, 11th and Guards armoured divisions. The battalion eliminated 40 enemy tanks, while on its own side it lost 3 King Tigers and 4 Tigers.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/t_26_mod_1933]
Just like several other countries, the USSR became interested in the Vickers Mk.E tank developed by the British company Vickers-Armstrongs Ltd. in 1928. In spring 1930, the Soviet delegation signed a contract for the supply of 15 Vickers Mk.E Type A tanks, including the designs for its mass production. Initially designated V-26 the tank was supplied to the Red Army in spring 1931 under the designation T-26 (the 1931 version). A total of 1,626 tanks of this type were manufactured in 1931-33. Some of the tanks were equipped with a 37 mm Hotchkiss gun installed in the right turret. 
Along with expanding mass production of the T-26 (the 1931 version), the design bureau headed by S.A. Ginsburg started the development of a single-turret version. Unlike Poland, which purchased the sets for upgrading their tanks to the Vickers Mk.E Type B standard, the Soviet designers developed the turret on their own. A 45 mm 20-K gun (the 1932 version) and a 7.62 mm DT coaxial machine gun were installed into the two-man cylindrical turret. This vehicle entered service under the designation “T-26, 1933 Version.” The first vehicles were manufactured in the summer of 1933. The tank was continuously improved during production: since 1936 the hulls were welded and a ball mount with a DT machine gun was installed in the rear. Part of the vehicles produced in 1937 had an anti-aircraft ring mount with a DT machine gun. Some tanks were equipped with radio stations and they could be identified by a stub antenna on top of the turret. Since 1938, the “T-26, 1938 Version” with a conic turret entered mass production. The final version of the tank was the “T-26, 1939 Version” with a re-shaped hull. A total of 10,117 tanks of the T-26 family were produced from 1931 until 1940.
The T-26 created in mid-1930s had become obsolete by June 1941. The slow and poorly armored T-26 was quite an easy target for German anti-tank guns. Yet, in a fight against enemy tanks (especially light tanks) the T-26 had a fair chance: its 45 mm gun round could penetrate the armor of most German tanks at the time.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/bt_7_mod_1935]
In 1934, an improved version of the wheel-track tank BT-5 was being developed. The new vehicle retained the turret of its predecessor’s (identical to the T-26 tank produced between 1934 and 1937) as well as armament but the hull design saw multiple changes. The hull front was welded unlike the BT-2 and BT-5 and the hull was up-armored. An M-17T engine was installed, which was the tank version of the M-17 aviation engine. Due to the installation of the new engine the exhaust pipes were modified, too; the same pipes appeared on the upgraded BT-5 tanks as well.
The first prototype of the new tank was produced in 1934 and mass production of the vehicle designated BT-7 began at the beginning of the following year. An upgraded version of the tank with a conic turret entered production in 1937, and a small-link track was added in 1938. In December 1930, the production of the final version of the BT-7 began, mainly known under the designation BT-7M (the tank was also designated BT-8). Unlike the previous BT version it was equipped with a V-2 diesel engine and 22-mm-thick reinforced frontal armor, yet its only visible difference was the air filter cap on the engine plate. A total of 5,328 tanks of the BT-7 family were produced including 155 BT-7A and 788 BT-7M tanks.
Like all wheel-track tanks, the main advantage of the BT-7 was its high speed. The BT-7 had a maximum road speed of 72 km/h (with tracks removed). The 45 mm gun was efficient against most enemy tanks. A major disadvantage of the BT-7 was its weak armor protection. It was this weak armor protection and the impossibility to strengthen it without major re-designs that led to a completely new design, the wheel-track tank BT-20, which evolved into the Т-34 later on.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/bt_7a]
The BT-7 was also used as a basis for the creation of an artillery support tank known under the designation BT-7A. It differed from the basic model due to a new turret with the short-barrel 76 mm KT-28 gun (the same gun was installed on the early versions of the T-28).  The hull was also slightly modified to accommodate the new turret. A total of 155 BT-7A tanks were produced from 1937 through 1938 and 134 of them were supplied to the army.
The BT-7A had the same strengths and weaknesses as the basic model.   The KT-28 gun was quite efficient against enemy infantry, but the armor protection was definitely insufficient for a support tank. This was probably the reason for the small number of the BT-7A manufactured.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/t_27]
In spring 1930, a Soviet delegation visited Great Britain and signed a contract with Vickers-Armstrongs Ltd. for the purchase of four types of armored vehicles. These included the Vickers Carden-Loyd Mk.IV tankette, which undoubtedly was a groundbreaking vehicle for its class. Along with the tankettes, the designs needed for their mass production were purchased. The Vickers Carden-Loyd Mk.IV was used as a basis for the development of the Soviet version of the tankette featuring a more powerful engine and armor protection, as well as armament consisting of a 7.62 mm DT machine gun. In February 1931, the tankette entered service under the designation T-27. A total of 3,297 tankettes were supplied to the troops between 1931 and 1934. 
By late 1930s, tankettes had become obsolete. The T-27 was noexception, being mostly used for training purposes. In 1941, the T-27 tankettes also served as towing vehicles for 45 mm anti-tank guns in addition to their traditional roles (reconnaissance and infantry support in some cases)

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/t_34_mod_1941]
In October 1937, Factory No. 183 (called the Kharkov Tractor Factory or KhTZ before 1936) was tasked with the development of a wheel-track tank which was to replace the BT-7 series. Upgrade the BT-7 hull was pointless; therefore, the tank with the factory designation BT-20 was designed from scratch borrowing only on the general layout and suspension of its predecessor. The prototype designated A-20 was produced in May 1939. Parallel to that, two prototypes designated A-32 were built on behalf of Mikhail Ilyich Koshkin, the chief designer of Factory No. 183, which represented a pure track version of the A-20. The two tanks featured different armament: the A-20 had a 45 mm gun (1938 version), whereas the A-32 had a 76 mm L-10 gun. The high maneuverability of the A-32 proved Koshkin right and the acceptance committee approved the development of the new version of the tank with increased armor (by 10 mm). 
The first two prototypes of the upgraded version of the A-32 designated A-34 were produced in January-February 1940. Koshkin died in September 1940, just at the time when his brainchild entered service under the designation T-34. Like the A-34, the new vehicle was armed with a 76 mm L-11 gun, which was the first timew that such a gun was installed on a medium tank. T-34 tanks were to be produced at two factories (Factory No. 183 and the Stalingrad Tractor Factory or STZ) but the production process took a long time to implement at STZ, so the tanks were mainly produced in Kharkov (117 vehicles in 1940). There were about four hundred T-34 tanks with the L-11 gun produced by March 1941, which are generally known under the designation “T-34, 1940 Version.” 
Since March 1941, production of the T-34 began with the 76 mm F-34 gun. This was the basic T-34 model when Germany attacked the USSR. A total of 1,225 tanks had been produced by July 1, 1941, STZ accounting for 294 of them and Factory No. 183 accounting for the other 931. Production of the T-34 tanks continued at Factory No. 183 through October with a total of 755 tanks produced. 
When the T-34 appeared, it was the best tank in its class. Because of its sloped armor plates the hull of the T-34 offered good protection against anti-tank shells while its powerful gun was able to kill enemy tanks at any distance. But it also had a number of weaknesses. The turret, which had only seen few changes from the time of the A-20, was cramped and could only house two crew members. Thus, in addition to his direct duties, the commander had to work as a loader. The turret offered restricted visibility and the front armor plate was weakened by the driver’s hatch(this weakness was only removed on the T-44). On top of that, the service life of the tank engine was only 150 hours. In spite of all its weaknesses the T-34 had a major strength: it was capable of fighting enemy tanks outside of the range of enemy shells. 

The T-28 was a Soviet multi-turreted medium tank. The prototype was completed in 1931, and production began in late 1932. It was an infantry support tank intended to break through fortified defences. The T-28 was designed to complement the heavier T-35 (also multi-turreted), with which it shared turret designs. The type did not have great success in combat, but it played an important role as a development project for Soviet tank designers. A series of new ideas and solutions that were tried out on the T-28 were later incorporated in future models.


[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/t_28_mod_1938]
Medium tank T-28

The T-28 is a medium tank of the interwar period. It was developed in 1930-1932. The T-28 is the first medium tank in the USSR launched into mass production. In the period from 1933 to 1940, the Leningrad Kirov Plant produced 503 copies of the T-28.

The T-28 was a three-turret medium tank of a classic layout, with cannon-machine gun armament and bulletproof armor, and was intended to support infantry and qualitative reinforcement of rifle and tank formations when breaking through enemy fortified positions. At the time of its appearance, the T-28 was the strongest medium tank in the world.

Since 1933, the T-28 tanks entered service with the heavy tank brigades of the Red Army, allocated since 1936 to the reserve of the Main Command. T-28 tanks were used in the Polish campaign of the Red Army and the Winter War, where they showed very high combat qualities. However, according to the results of the battles on the Karelian Isthmus, the armor protection of the tanks was found insufficient, as a result of which some of the tanks were subjected to additional shielding. By the middle of 1941, the T-28 was morally obsolete, but in terms of its tactical and technical characteristics it still surpassed almost all tank models available to the Wehrmacht. As part of the tank brigades of the mechanized corps of the Red Army, the T-28 participated in the battles of the initial period of the Great Patriotic War, but most of them were lost during its first months, mainly due to technical malfunctions. The last combat use of the T-28 by Red Army units was recorded in 1944.

Captured T-28s were used by the Finnish troops, which were armed with these tanks until 1951.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/t_30]
T-30 (factory index 030, Т-60 name was intended to be used in the army) — non-amphibious more armored variant without screw propeller recess. Some late production vehicles were armed with experimental 20mm gun ShVAK-T. Additional perfection of hull and armament (discarding floatable hull, ShVAK-T revision) led to creation of T-60 light tank, which was armed with 20mm TNSh gun.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/t_40s]
In late 1938, development of a small-sized amphibious tank began with the goal to replace the T-38. The prototype designated “0-10” was fundamentally different from the earlier amphibious tanks. The tank had a high trapezoid hull in its cross-section, individual torsion bar suspension, and a conic tower with a 12.7 mm DShK machine gun and a 7.62 mm DT machine gun. The re-designed version “0-10” entered service in 1939 under the designation T-40 with a total of 374 tanks produced. 
Due to the urgent need for armored vehicles in summer 1940, a simplified version of the T-40 entered production which lacked the screw propeller in the rear. Such tanks are known under the designation T-40S, i.e. the land version. The rear compartment was removed in August 1941 and in the following month some of the vehicles were being armed with 20 mm TNSh (ShVAK) guns. Such vehicles are known under the designation T-30. 
Designed as a reconnaissance tank, the T-40 was the best tank in the world in its class when it appeared. Unfortunately, due to a general lack of armored vehicles this vehicle was often used as a line tank, which was an unusual role for it.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/T_60]
T-60 Light Tank model 1942

The light T-60 tank was designed in the summer 1941 on the base of the T-40 tank, during a great shortage of time and resources. The new tank design was to have used the tested and proven T-40 design, differing from it principally only in its more powerful weaponry and armour. The vehicle was specially designed in such a way as to allow its production on underequipped plants, at automobile factories in particular. The serial production of the tank began in October of 1941, at Moscow Plant No. 37 (which was later evacuated to Sverdlovsk where production continued). The T-60 was also produced at Plants No. 38 (Kirov), 254 (Sarepta) and at the GAZ.

The T-60 was a light tank whose armour provided protection mainly from bullets. Only its front armour could protect it from small-calibre automatic guns, and in some cases from 37mm Pak 35/36 antitank gun projectiles. The first vehicles had hardened armour, which was later fortified when the thickness of the front armour plate of the hull and the turret was increased from 25mm to 35mm. The primary weapon of the tank, the 20mm TNSh automatic gun, was a modification of the ShVAK aircraft gun. Its main drawback was its relatively weak ballistics, which led to poor armour-piercing qualities. It could engage only light-armoured vehicles: personnel carriers, armour cars, and light tanks. Altogether, the T-60 corresponded roughly in its capabilities to the German light tank Pz.II Ausf F. Serial production of the T-60 made evident the fact that the vehicle did not meet the requirements for a light tank - but there was no other choice. The army needed tanks desperately, and the alternative T-50 light tank failed to reach production. The main advantage of the T-60 (a classic product of the mobilization economy) was the simplicity of its manufacture and its ability to be produced at non-specialized facilities.

The T-60 entered action for the first time at the Battle of Moscow. The majority of them were in service in 1942, and by 1943 their numbers had reduced drastically due to heavy losses, but they still were not uncommon. For example, on June 4, 1943 there were sixty tanks of this type on the Voronezh Front. A small number of these tanks remained in the field even later, although they generally played supporting roles: headquarters security, convoy escort, and they served as command vehicles in SU-76 regiments.

By 1942, the T-60 had begun to be gradually replaced by the T-70 tank; by the end of that year this conversion was all but complete, and the last small batch of T-60 tanks rolled off the line at Plant No. 37 in January, 1943, after a total of 5,920 T-60 tanks had been produced.


[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/T_70M]
T-70M Light Tank model 1942

The works on a T-60 light tank modification started virtually as soon as its design was finished, in October 1941. Being aware that the tank's main drawback was its weaponry, it was decided to mount a 45mm gun on the tank. But the new gun required a larger and heavier turret, which led to increasing the tank weight and overloading its engine. The difficulty was solved by using a propulsion system of two coaxial GAZ-202 engines, which led in turn to hull enlargements and strengthening of the running gear. As the result of this, the new tank designed at GAZ and called T-70 differed rather greatly from its ancestor. The trial of the tank took place in the beginning of 1942; the development prototype had a cast turret. As a whole the trials were successful, but the request had been made to harden armouring and implement a two-man turret. While the first request was fulfilled in a relatively short time (the thickness of the upper front armour plate was increased to 45mm), the two-man turret was implemented only in the newer T-80 tank. The T-70 had a rival. T-45 tank designed at Plant No. 37 had several advantages over the T-70 - wider usage of the T-60's in-process stock, lesser weight and cost, single-engine propulsion system, but eventually work on the T-45 was terminated. The serial production of engines for that model were only getting up and running, while the production of T-70 had already been mastered, and the tank could be further improved. On March 6, 1942 T-70 was accepted for service and its serial production began. The first tanks were dispatched to the front as soon as April 1942.

During 1942 work on T-70 modernization was conducted, it was primarily focused on mounting a new two-man turret. Among other things, the new heavier turret required running gear enhancement; there were other minor changes as well. It was proposed to implement those changes (excluding the turret) in T-70s produced in the autumn of 1942, which would allow immediate switching to the production of a new design once the turret would have been adapted. The proposal was accepted, and in October 1942 a new modification designated T-70M was put into serial production. The production of the original T-70 modification was terminated in the spring of 1943. Eventually a two-man turret was implemented in the T-80 tank, which was a modernization of the T-70. The T-80 was accepted for service in December 1942.

At a whole, T-70 met its requirements as of 1942. Its front armour provided decent protection from still widespread German 37mm guns, and in some cases even from 50mm guns, short-barrel especially. 45mm gun allowed the tank to successfully oppose German armoured vehicles. But in 1943 the situation had changed. The German army was saturated with powerful 50mm and 75mm antitank guns, and the most common German tanks, Pz.III and Pz.IV, got enhanced front armour and became a hard target for 45mm guns. Introducing APCR projectiles to tank ammunition alleviated the problem to a certain degree, but the charges were effective only at a close range. The tank was evidently outdated, and after the Battle of Kursk its production began to scale down. The last vehicles were produced in December, 1943.

T-70 and T-70M tanks took an active part in battles of the summer of 1942 - 1943, a good many of them were still in use in 1944, and some vehicles (those used as command in self-propelled artillery regiments) fought in 1945 as well. The pinnacle of its combat application (and the beginning of its decline) was the Battle of Kursk. For example, as of June 4, 1943 there were 345 T-70 and T-70M under command of Voronezh Front. As of July 11, 1943 5th Guard Tank Army had 318 T-70 and T-70M tanks, 563 T-34 tanks and 28 Churchill tanks on the roll.

A total of 8,231 T-70 and T-70M were produced by GAZ and Plant No. 37 and 38.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/t_34_57]
3 June 1940 head of Defense Committee S. Timoshenko sent the staff report about inadequate weapon power of new T-34 and KV tanks and other promising tanks in development to the Central Committee of Communist Party and SNK (Council of People Commissars) of USSR and 27 June special conference of SNK and Central Committee of Communist Party took place regarding this matter. It was agreed that 45mm tank guns are too weak and unpromising, new 55-60mm tank guns should be developed instead.
Experimental prototype of new 57mm gun was made and tests begun in the early December. Factory tests on field gun-carriage took place from December to end of March 1941 and in April it was installed into T-34 for field testing, which revealed short barrel life and low grouping of shots. In July 1941 elaborated gun, designated as ZIS-4, was again installed into T-34 and new tests took place on Sophrino firing range. They were successful, but technology of making such long barrels was extremely expensive and the new gun was approved only because need for powerful anti-tank guns was considered paramount. These guns was intended for installing on special “tanks-destroyers” T-34-57 and mass production of ZIS-4 was started on factory #92 in the middle of July 1941, as ordered by NKV (People Commissariat of Weapons).
Т-34-57 saw action in Battle for Moscow. For example, 21st tank brigade formed in Vladimir had 10 of these tanks. At 14th October tank brigade was unloaded on rail station Demidovo and on the following day it was ordered to advance on Turchinovo-Pushkino-Troyanovo route to make flank attack into group of German forces near Kalinin. These fights uncovered new heroes: tank under command of “politruk” (ideology officer) Gmyrya arrived at Volokolamsk highway when large column of German cars and infantry was moving along it. The tank devastated entire column (2-3km long!), then it rushed in German airfield and destroyed heavy bomber using main gun. After that tank was knocked out by fire from AT and plane guns, but Gmyrya and his sergeant Ischenko fought their way through to Soviet forces. During 4 days fighting, brigade destroyed 3 headquarters, up to 1000 soldiers, 34 tanks, 210 cars, 25 AT guns, 6 “thermite guns” and others. Tank regiment commander of this brigade, Hero of the Soviet Union major Lukin and 1st tank battalion commander, Hero of the Soviet Union captain Abigalov died in these fights. All T-34-57 were lost before 25th November.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/T_34_43_Kursk1943]
T-34 Command Tank of Plant No. 183, model 1943

The final version of the T-34 mounting a 76mm gun was put into serial production at the onset of  summer in 1943. Its most significant external difference was a command pod, which was originally proposed by the design bureau of Plant No. 112 and which was later used universally in tank construction. The command pod significantly improved the visibility of T-34 (which was previously its long-standing shortcoming), though it increased the time required for the crew to evacuate from a dangerously compromised tank. In addition, these tanks received cylindrical external fuel tanks, which greatly increased their radius of action. In the spring of 1943, production of a new five-speed gearbox was mastered, which significantly increased the tank's response characteristics. All these innovations were introduced during production, primarily at the Ural Tank Plant (UTP) (designated Plant No. 183 and situated in Nizhny Tagil, the principal production centre for T-34 tanks). A total of 15,013 T-34 tanks were produced there from 1941 to 1944, two and a half times as many than were produced at Plant No. 112 in Gorky.

These plants also produced command modifications to the T-34, units designed for tank company commanders and high command. These tanks could be identified by the second periscope on the turret, and so a second false periscope (essentially was a piece of a steel pipe) was mounted on the other battle tanks beginning in the summer of 1943, in order to prevent the enemy from singling out the commander tanks on the battlefield. Few command-pod T-34 tanks took part in the Battle of Kursk, as its production had only just begun.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/T_34_zd_112_Kursk1943]
T-34 Medium Tank of Plant No. 112, model 1943

Plant No. 112 (the Krasnoe Sormovo Factory in Gorky) began production of T-34 tanks as early as September 1941. Werial production was hindered by a shortage of essential machinery and materials. For example, due to the insufficient supply of V-2 diesel engines, 696 T-34 tanks were manufactured with M-17 gasoline engines at this plant in 1941 and 1942. Until the summer of 1943, tanks produced at Plant No. 112 were of the lowest quality compared to tanks produced in other plants. Assembly was negligent, the welding quality of joints was low (as the plant lacked semiautomatic welding devices), tanks were incomplete, and so on. In 1943, the peculiarity of Sormovo Plant's tanks was the so-called tight cast turret, based on an older design (owing to the fact that the foundry processes at the plant were also outdated). Other plants had already begun production of hexagonal cast turrets.

The situation changed in the summer of 1943. The plant received new machinery, semiautomatic welding devices in particular, and besides, a group of engineers arrived from other plants. As a result, Plant No. 112 pioneered the implementation of many innovations. For example, it was in Plant No. 112 that a new command pod for the T-34 was designed. A total of 6,274 T-34 tanks were produced between 1941 and 1944.

By 1943, the T-34 had lost its advantage in weaponry and armour over the German tanks. The T-34's gun could not reliably penetrate the front armour plate of the newly modified Pz.III and Pz.IV, let alone the Panthers and Tigers, and at the same time German 50mm and 75mm long antitank and tank guns could pierce the armour of a T-34 at realistic combat range, although the upper front armour plate could sometimes protect T-34 from these threats. An old problem of the T-34, its low visibility, was solved by the summer of 1943, with the introduction of a command pod, but the functional overload of the commander (who was required to act as gunner at the same time) remained a problem. In addition, the tank's weaponry required significant modernization. These problems were resolved by the model T-34-85 tanks.

The T-34 was the backbone of the Soviet tank forces in the Battle of Kursk. There were 1,861 tanks under command of the Voronezh Front by July 4, 1943; of these, 1,161 were T-34 tanks.


[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/T_34_zd_174_Kursk1943]
T-34 Medium Tank of Plant No. 174, model spring 1943

Originally, plant No. 174, evacuated first from Leningrad to Chkalov (Orenburg), and then to Omsk, was not intended for T-34 production. The plant was equipped for a light tank T-50 production, and by August 1942 the plant had prepared the assembly line for this model, which was in high demand. 

However, production of the T-50 had never began, as the plant failed to get its engine production up and running. Besides, after the advancement of German troops to Stalingrad, the production of the T-34 had drastically decreased, and it was decided that, as a temporary measure, its production would be handled by the ChKZ (which had produced only heavy tanks KV before that) and Plant No. 174. The production of the T-34 at Omsk was difficult to organize, and the construction quality was low. Some parts, cast turrets in particular, the plant received from suppliers. In spite of this, a total of 417 T-34 tanks were produced in Omsk in 1942, 1,347 more in 1943, 2,163 in 1944 and 1,940 in 1945.


[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/T_34_zd_183_Kursk1943]
T-34 Medium Tank of Plant No. 183, model spring 1943 with a pressed turret

In the autumn of 1941, the principal tank construction centre of the T-34, the Kharkov Plant No. 183, was evacuated to Ural. In addition, the Mariupol Metallurgical Plant and the Moscow Machine Tool Plant were housed in the workshops of Uralvagonzavod in Nizhny Tagil. The new plant was renamed the Stalin Ural Tank Factory (UTZ), retaining its designation as No. 183. In December 1941, in the face of enormous difficulties, the plant began tank assembly on the basis of evacuated process stock. This new enterprise was fortuitous for several reasons. Firstly, the Institute of electric welding had been evacuated to Nizhny Tagil as well, which made it possible to begin the automatic welding of hull elements as early as January 1941 - and by the end of November of 1942 there were a total of six semiautomatic welding devices at the UTZ. Secondly, Uralvagonzavod had a great enough capacity to organize foundry production, which aided in the speedy preparation of cast turret production. Before this time, turrets had been supplied by Uralmash (UZTM) from Sverdlovsk. Thirdly, serial production was overseen by the evacuated design bureau.

It was at the UTZ, in the spring of 1942, that a new and easier to manufacture hexagonal cast turret (called the 'nut') was designed. Eventually it was accepted at other plants as well. The manpower and production cost of tank production had been significantly decreased. The vehicles produced at the UTZ were considered to be of the highest quality; and it was the UTZ which produced the reference tanks which were tested in Great Britain and the USA. By the beginning of 1943, a new five-speed gearbox for the T-34 was designed and put to production, which significantly increased the tank's responsiveness and eased the driver’s duties.

Pressed turrets produced at the UZTM in Sverdlovsk were mounted on some UTZ tanks. Those turrets had been produced since October 1942 with the assistance of a 10,000 ton press, which had previously been used for turbine bucket production. It should be noted that pressed turrets provided far better protection than cast turrets. A total of 2,670 pressed turrets had been produced at the UZTM by March 1944. Those turrets were mounted on their own tanks as well as on the T-34 tanks produced at Plants No. 183 and 112.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/T_34_85]
T-34-85 M1944 Medium Tank

By the spring of 1943, Soviet military leaders had analyzed the performance of their own guns against a German Tiger tank which had been captured near Leningrad. The results proved the inefficiency of existing Russian anti-tank and tank guns, and spurred the development of more powerful weaponry. Initially, the 85mm tank gun, with the ballistics of the M1939 anti-aircraft gun, was developed for KV and IS heavy tanks, as well as for their self-propelled guns. In addition, the T-34’s anti-tank capabilities were strengthened by a 57mm gun with improved ballistics. Further, plans were drawn up to begin production of a new T-43 medium tank, which was later to be rearmed with the 85mm gun. However, by the fall of 1943 a new plan was developed: rearming T-34 with the 85mm gun, although this would require a new tank turret. Eventually both the T-43 and T-34 with the 57mm gun were cancelled in favor of a new T-34 variant, designated T-34-85.
However, the D-5T gun, developed for heavy tanks and initially mounted on the T-34-85, did not entirely meet the requirements, primarily because it had a longer breech. This spurred the development of a new tank gun, which concluded with the successful field tests of the S-53 gun. The first T-34-85 tanks, built early in 1944, were armed with the earlier D-5T, but this gun was later quickly replaced by the more compact and more easily-manufacturable S-53. Aside from better armaments, T-34-85 received a new, more spacious, and better armored three-seat tank turret, which solved the long-standing issue of T-34 tank commanders being responsible for too many tasks.
The T-34-85 found extensive action in the World War II, and by early 1945, these tanks had nearly replaced all earlier T-34 variants which carried the smaller 76mm guns. But by the end of the war, the T-34-85's armor, especially its hull armor, could no longer provide adequate protection from enemy’s ever more destructive anti-tank and tank guns. Nevertheless, its own powerful armaments and high mobility, combined with the sharp tactics of its commanders, still brought the successful completion of key missions.
The T-34-85 was still being mass-produced during the postwar years, and was licensed for manufacture in Poland and Czechoslovakia. Thus the T-34-85 became one of the most popular – and after much filming, the most renowned – tanks in the world. As the main Soviet tank, the T-34-85 was succeeded by the T-54.
The T-34-85 tanks provided to North Korea by the Soviet Union were used extensively in the Korean War. These tanks established a good reputation, especially at the early stages of the conflict, when their mere arrival on the battlefield was able to spread panic among the enemy forces. The US forces were relieved by the arrival of their Sherman tanks, which were similar to the T-34-85, as well as their more powerful M26 and M46 tanks. That said, the Korean War did not see many tank duels, owing to the low number of North Korean tanks and the adverse terrain of the area. Indeed, infantry armed with Super Bazookas were the greater threat to the T-34-85.
T-34-85 tanks were shipped to tens of countries besides North Korea, and fought in numerous armed conflicts around the globe. In some countries, a number of these vehicles remained in serviceable condition into the 21st century.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/kv_1_f32]
During Autumn 1938 Leningrad Kirov Factory (LKZ) was ordered to design a heavy multi-turreted tank of second generation to replace T-35. It was quickly realized that specified conditions (maximum mass 60 tons, 60mm armor thickness) limit number of turrets to 2 maximum. Two prototypes, SMK and T-100 were built and tested in battle in Finnish 1939-1940 Winter war. Both of them were not mass-produced. Simultaneously with 2-turret tanks single turret variant was developed and designated KV (Klim Voroshilov). It was based on SMK design, but constructors decreased hull dimensions considerably, increasing armor thickness. KV-1 prototype, U-0, had two guns: 45mm tank gun mod.1934 and 76mm tank gun L-11. During trials which begun in September 1939 45mm gun was removed and DT machinegun was installed instead, with this armament U-0 was battle-tested in Winter war. First tank of initial series, U-2, was released for army testing in February 1940.
KV tanks of initial series had chamfered welded turrets, while on mass produced ones more simple cut welded turrets were fitted. 76mm L-11 tank guns were installed on KV tanks until December 1940 (142 tanks were made). Contrary to popular opinion that these tanks didn't see combat, there are photos which prove they did. Beginning in January 1941, new 76.2mm F-32 tank guns with better ballistics were installed in KV tanks (now called KV-1), this variant became most numerous when the war broke out. 423 tanks were made before 1st July 1941 on both LTZ and ChTZ (Chelyabinsk Tractor Factory). Another new gun, 76mm ZiS-5, was used in KV-1 tanks during Autumn 1941. Because of armor plates shortage, KV-1 tanks with cast turrets were mass-produced along with KV-1 with welded turrets starting in January 1942.
KV-1 was the best heavy tank in the world until Pz.Kpfw.VI Ausf.E Tiger was made. Contrary to other heavy tanks of it's time, KV-1 had not only excellent armor, but a good weaponry as well, enabling it to combat enemy infantry and armor effectively. Unfortunately, hasty adoption into service resulted in poor reliability of the tank, especially of it's chassis. Large percentage of KV-1 was lost because of technical difficulties, in addition Red Army lacked trained crews. Nevertheless, with good crew KV-1 was a formidable weapon, during 1941 Germans could effectively use only 88mm Flak 18/36 and 50mm AT guns Pak 38 against it.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/Kv_1]
KV-1 Heavy Tank model 1941

In the spring of 1938, design began on a new heavy tank, which was intended to replace the outdated T-35. In 1939, production began on development prototypes designated the SMK and T-100 started, both of which had two turrets. Besides these two vehicles, design of a single-turret heavy tank based on the SMK and designated the KV was launched in February of 1939. Testing of the KV began in September 1939, at which time the tank was equipped with 45mm and 76mm guns on the single turret. After the beginning of the Finnish War, these prototypes were dispatched to the front for in-field tests. These tests revealed the advantage of the single turret KV-1. In December of 1939, this model was adopted for service, and its serial production began at the Kirov Plant in Leningrad. Though its 1940 run was limited to 139 KV-1 tanks and 104 KV-2 tanks, in January of 1941 the Chelyabinsk Tractor Plant (ChTZ) joined the production of KV.

The KV-1 had some modifications, including variation of the gun between three different models (the L-11, F-32 and ZiS-5), the turret (cast-in or weld fabricated), armour panels, and so on. Also produced were the assault tank KV-2, which was equipped with a 152mm howitzer, and the flame-throwing KV-8 tank.

In 1941, the KV's armour provided a reliable protection against the Wehrmacht's antitank and tank artillery, and its armour-piercing projectiles could successfully engage enemy armoured vehicles. This gave experienced tank crews an advantage on the battlefield. In 1941, the KV-1 was as dangerous to Germans forces as the Tiger would become for Soviet forces in 1943. German troops were driven to use 88mm AA and 105mm field guns to fend off this Soviet threat.
In 1942, the situation began to change. German forces received 75mm and 76 mm towed and self-propelled guns with good ballistic capability, and their 50mm antitank and tank guns were widely using APCR projectiles. Then, in 1943 when the Tiger and Panther tanks and 88mm antitank guns appeared, the armour of the KV completely lost its former superiority. Along with that, its weaponry did not enable it to reliably penetrate the front armour plate of the latest Pz.III and Pz.IV modifications, let alone the Tigers and Panthers.

The shortcomings of the tank were its huge weight, unreliable transmission, limited field of vision, and the tank commander's functional overload (for he also had to carry out loader duty). A new modification, the KV-1s, which was designed in the summer of 1942, resolved these problems, but at the cost of weaker armour. In July of 1942 the ChTZ produced the last KV-1 tanks.

KV-1 was widely used since the first days of the war, especially on the Leningrad front. Many vehicles of this type were in use in 1942 as well. In 1943 a significant part of KV-1 tanks were replaced by the KV-1s, but nevertheless the earlier model remained a common fixture of the field. They fought in the Battle of Kursk as well: for example there were nine KV-1 and one KV-1s in the 203rd Separate Tank Regiment.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/kv_2]
In late 1939, an assault tank based on the KV was designed, armed with a 152 mm M-10 howitzer (the 1938 version). Besidesthe armament the new vehicle differed in its larger turret designated MT-1. The tank was primarily designed to destroy enemy fortifications. The first tank to receive the MT-1 turret was the U-0 tank (the KV prototype) after its return from combat testing. In February three vehicles (U-0, U-1 and U-3) were sent for combat testing and participated in breaking through the Mannerheim Line. The first samples had a special muzzle brake, but it was frequently torn off during the tests and rejected. 
Mass production of the tank initially designated “big turret KV” began in early spring 1940. The first 46 tanks were equipped with MT-1 turrets. In fall of that year an improved variant of the tank was designed with a lowered turret and this version became the main model of this series. A total of 167 tanks with a lowered turret were produced until July 1941 (in 1941 they were designated KV-2). 
As mentioned above, the KV-2 was intended to destroy the enemy fortifications, primarily pillboxes. In a situation when the Soviet tanks had to defend rather than attack, there was no longer any need for the KV-2 tanks and their production stopped. This should not imply, however, that the KV-2 was a bad tank; it suffices to say that after point-blank hits of the 152 mm shell, enemy tanks would literally crumble with their welded seams breaking apart. Just like with the KV-1, the major weakness of the KV-2 was its unreliability, especially its running gear.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/Kv_1C_Kursk1943]
KV-1s Heavy Tank model 1942

The KV-1 heavy tank had several drawbacks, which made maintenance difficult and which decreased its combat effectiveness. Among them there were:

- considerable weight (47.5 tons) which significantly hindered the vehicle's mobility
- unreliable transmission
- low efficiency of the engine cooling system
- poor turret visibility

In June of 1942, Soviet High Command ordered the design of a KV tank modification, free of these shortcomings. In July of 1942, work on these new vehicles began, and on August 20, 1942, the KV-1s (high speed) tanks were adopted for service and put into serial production.

The KV-1s weighed 42.3 tons, but this weight-reduction cost a heavy price, for the thickness of its armour plates was reduced from 70mm to 60mm. A new, smaller cast-in turret with a command pod was mounted on the tank, which also received a new gearbox and friction coupling, improved engine cooling system, and lightened chassis. The characteristics of the KV-1s were similar to those of the T-34. It featured the same weaponry, and its armour somewhat excelled the armour of the latter. Its doubtless advantage over the T-34 was the presence of a dedicated commander and a command pod, as well as the equipment of all tanks with radio sets. It must be noted, however, that its armour did not provide protection from the most common antitank and tank ordnances of the Wehrmacht. For example, the 50mm Pak 38 gun could penetrate the KV-1s side armour from a range of 500m, and the 75mm Pak 40 gun was able to pierce the front armour from a range of 1000m. In addition, the gun of the KV-1s could not reliably penetrate the front armour of the new Pz.III and Pz.IV modifications, let alone the Tigers and Panthers. This resulted in the termination of the production of KV-1s and the KV-85 variant model, which was a KV-1s equipped with an 85mm gun.

KV-1s tanks were the backbone of the Separate Guards tank regiments, which were further reinforced by twenty-one KV or 'Churchill' tanks. One KV-1s tank was reserved for the commander of the self-propelled artillery regiment of the SU-152 (an SP gun mounted on a KV-1s chassis). Other KV-1s tanks were dispatched to regular tank brigades. The KV-1s saw its widest use from the autumn of 1942 through 1943, but later their numbers in field decreased significantly due to losses, though some remained in action even into 1945. By July 4, 1943, the 203rd separate tank regiment, at the Voronezh front, had twenty-four KV-1 and KV-1s tanks. The regiment was fighting in the direction of Oboyan. Another twenty-four KV tanks were available to the 262nd Separate tank regiment and one such tank was under charge of the 1529th self-propelled tank artillery regiment.

The serial production of the KV-1s was terminated in August 1943, before which time a total of 1,370 vehicles of this type had been produced at the Chelyabinsk Kirov Plant (ChKP).

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/Kv_85]
KV-85 M1943 Heavy Tank

A stopgap upgrade to the KV series was the short-lived KV-85 or Objekt 239. This was a KV-1S with the new turret from the Object 237 (IS-85) still in development, mounting the same 85 mm D-5T gun as the SU-85 and early versions of the T-34-85 (not yet in production at the time). The 85 mm proved capable of penetrating the Tiger I from 1000 m and the demand for it slowed production of the KV-85 tremendously, only 148 were built between August and October 1943. Soviet industry was therefore able to produce a heavy tank as well armed as the Tiger I before the end of 1943. Although the KV-85 was an excellent opponent to the Tigers and Panthers, it was a stopgap and thus was built in small numbers. The complete Object 237 was accepted into service as the IS-85 and was produced in the autumn and winter of 1943-44; they were sent to the front as of October 1943 and production of the IS-85/IS-1 was stopped by the spring of 1944 once the IS-122/IS-2 entered full-scale production.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/is_1]
IS-1 M1943 Heavy Tank

IS-1 heavy tank was developed in design bureau of Kirov factory in Chelyabinsk under direction of Zh.Y. Kotin in second half of 1942. It was based on KV-13 (as well as IS-2).
IS-1 had 76mm gun, while IS-2 was armed with 122mm howitzer-gun. First experimental IS prototypes had 5-rollers suspension as well as KV-13, and looked much like it.
First IS-1 variant with 85mm gun D-5T (IS-85 or “Object 237”) was developed during Summer 1943 in the same design bureau under direction of  lead designer N.F. Shamshurin. It was necessary two add two more rollers to suspension and more powerful engine to cope with increased weight of armament. It was designated IS-1 (or IS-85) andofficially added to Soviet arsenal at 7th August by order of GKO (State Defense Committee).
It weighted 44 tons, not much more than KV, but had thicker armor. Turret, front hull, turret support and rear were cast. IS-1 hull width was 18cm smaller than KV-1S width thanks to installation of smaller planetary rotation mechanisms.
Production of IS-1 tanks was cancelled in the beginning of 1944.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/is_2_early]
IS-2 M1943 Heavy Tank

The development of IS tanks started early in 1942, as a part of the KV tank improvement program, which strengthened conventional armor with very thick molded pieces of iron. Work proceeded on what was then designated the KV-13, but progress was slow: the project saw a number of changes, with many kinds of armaments being considered. Although it would mean yet more costly re-engineering, in the spring of 1943 it was decided that 85mm guns would be the ideal armament, as they were able to stand against even heavily armored German tanks. Finally, by the fall of 1943, the first of these vehicles had been built and tested, and the line entered mass production. Alas, by that time, the 85mm gun had already become insufficient for a heavy tank.
The Battle of Kursk proved 85mm armaments incapable of inflicting sufficient damage on the well-armored German tanks - the Pantera’s front armor in particular. This was, however, an easy task for 122mm A-19 corps guns. Consequently, development began on a 122mm tank gun, with ballistic features similar to those of the A-19. Already in September 1943, an experimental IS tank with a 122mm gun entered field tests. After a number of improvements, it was finally adopted in December of 1943, and entered mass production to replace the IS tanks armed with the 85mm gun. Initially, the new tank was designated IS-122, but was later renamed the IS-2.
The IS-2 had heavy armor that provided exceptional protection against the widespread 75mm German anti-tank and tank guns. This armor became even more resilient after the introduction of flat glacis plate. The tank’s armor could sometimes even withstand 88mm gun fire. Its 122mm gun was the most powerful mass-produced tank gun of World War II. Its ballistic features provided for efficient damage against the enemy’s mass-produced armored vehicles, and its heavy high-explosive shells could easily demolish buildings and fieldwork. On the downside, its rate of fire was low due to separate ammo, low ammo capacity, extended gun muzzle brake, and long gun overhang, as well as low unit power. Despite these drawbacks, the IS-2 was widely used by Soviets in the World War II, and established an excellent reputation in a wide range of combat missions, from breaching anti-tank defenses to urban combat and tank duels. These tanks were assigned to separate tank regiments, 21 tanks each.
During the Korean War, IS-2 tanks were adopted by the Chinese, but they saw limited use and few specifics are known. By the end of the war, the Chinese forces in Korea are known to have had 38 IS-2 tanks. In the 1960s, these tanks were also sent to Cuba and North Korea. Late in the 1950s, the IS-2 was upgraded and adopted by the Soviet Army for a long period of time. Some of them were converted into emplacements along the Chinese border.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/is_2]
IS-2 M1943 Heavy Tank

In early 1942, the design bureau of the Kirov Factory in Chelyabinsk (ChKZ) started developing a tank designated KV-13. In fact, this was a brand-new vehicle which had next to nothing from its predecessor, the KV-1. Moreover, this tank was to be classified as a medium rather than heavy tank in terms of weight. Weighing 30 tons, the KV-13 was to have 75-mm-thick armor, a crew of 3, and armament consisting of the 76 mm ZIS-5 gun and the 7.62 mm DT coaxial machine gun. The vehicle seemed to be quite simple in production, which was important in a situation when tanks were needed badly. Unfortunately, the KV-13 prototype produced in spring 1942 proved extremely unreliable. Further tests were delayed as was the production of the second prototype with an up-armored hull (this brought the vehicle’s weight up to 38 tons). 
In January 1943 two samples of the German Pz.Kpfw.VI Ausf.H1 Tiger heavy tank were captured. Shooting tests carried out in April that year showed that the 76 mm F-34 and ZIS-5 tank guns could only penetrate the German vehicle’s armor at point-blank range (at a distance of up to 100-200 meters). The best-suited gun for destroying the Tiger was the 85 mm 52-K anti-aircraft cannon and the 122 mm A-19 howitzer. Neither gun would fit into the turrets of the tanks which were in serviceat the time, so new designs were needed. One of them was the KV-13 design which was renamed to IS (for Iosef Stalin) on February 24, 1943. In spring of 1943, two samples of the IS tank were produced: the IS-1 with a three-man turret and a 76 mm F-34M gun and the IS-2 with the 122 mm U-11 howitzer installed in the turret of the KV-9 tank prototype. In summer 1943, testing of the IS prototype armed with the 85 mm D-5T gun began. In September, this vehicle entered service under the designation IS-85. 
Meanwhile, in fall 1943, the 122 mm D-25T gun was developed consisting of a barrel from the 122 mm A-19 howitzer combined with the gun-cradle and the recoil mechanism of the U-11 howitzer. To decrease recoil, the barrel was equipped with a muzzle brake (during the production run the shape of the muzzle brake was changed several times). The tank armed with the D-25T gun successfully passed all tests and entered service under the designation IS-122. In March 1944, the IS-85 was re-designated IS-1 whereas the IS-122 was re-designated IS-2. During the production run the IS-2 design underwent a number of changes, the biggest of which was the remodeling of the hull front plate. A total of 3,483 tanks of this type were produced.
The IS-2 was undoubtedly the best Soviet heavy tank of WWII. But it is not quite appropriate to classify it as a heavy tank: weighing 46 tons it was only about a ton heavier than the Pz.Kpfw.V Panther referred to by the Germans as medium tank. The Pz.Kpfw.VI Ausf.H1 Tiger was 10 tons heavier than the IS-2, but the Soviet tank had better armor protection. Along with its advantages the IS-2 had its disadvantages, too, mainly regarding the low rate of fire of the D-25T gun (only 2-3 rounds per minute) and the poor quality of the driver sight. In theory, the armor-piercing round fired from the D-25T gun penetrated a 115-mm-thick plate, but tests quicklyshowed that this had been somewhat overestimated. Luckily as of summer 1944 the quality of German armor began to decrease sharply. The tanks of the early production series had weaker hull front armor, but this problem was solved in the summer of 1944, when production of hulls with the flattened front armor plate began.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/IS_2]
IS-2 M1944 Heavy Tank

The development of IS tanks started early in 1942, as a part of the KV tank improvement program, which strengthened conventional armor with very thick molded pieces of iron. Work proceeded on what was then designated the KV-13, but progress was slow: the project saw a number of changes, with many kinds of armaments being considered. Although it would mean yet more costly re-engineering, in the spring of 1943 it was decided that 85mm guns would be the ideal armament, as they were able to stand against even heavily armored German tanks. Finally, by the fall of 1943, the first of these vehicles had been built and tested, and the line entered mass production. Alas, by that time, the 85mm gun had already become insufficient for a heavy tank.
The Battle of Kursk proved 85mm armaments incapable of inflicting sufficient damage on the well-armored German tanks - the Pantera’s front armor in particular. This was, however, an easy task for 122mm A-19 corps guns. Consequently, development began on a 122mm tank gun, with ballistic features similar to those of the A-19. Already in September 1943, an experimental IS tank with a 122mm gun entered field tests. After a number of improvements, it was finally adopted in December of 1943, and entered mass production to replace the IS tanks armed with the 85mm gun. Initially, the new tank was designated IS-122, but was later renamed the IS-2.
The IS-2 had heavy armor that provided exceptional protection against the widespread 75mm German anti-tank and tank guns. This armor became even more resilient after the introduction of flat glacis plate. The tank’s armor could sometimes even withstand 88mm gun fire. Its 122mm gun was the most powerful mass-produced tank gun of World War II. Its ballistic features provided for efficient damage against the enemy’s mass-produced armored vehicles, and its heavy high-explosive shells could easily demolish buildings and fieldwork. On the downside, its rate of fire was low due to separate ammo, low ammo capacity, extended gun muzzle brake, and long gun overhang, as well as low unit power. Despite these drawbacks, the IS-2 was widely used by Soviets in the World War II, and established an excellent reputation in a wide range of combat missions, from breaching anti-tank defenses to urban combat and tank duels. These tanks were assigned to separate tank regiments, 21 tanks each.
During the Korean War, IS-2 tanks were adopted by the Chinese, but they saw limited use and few specifics are known. By the end of the war, the Chinese forces in Korea are known to have had 38 IS-2 tanks. In the 1960s, these tanks were also sent to Cuba and North Korea. Late in the 1950s, the IS-2 was upgraded and adopted by the Soviet Army for a long period of time. Some of them were converted into emplacements along the Chinese border.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/IS_3]
IS-3 M1945 Heavy Tank

The Soviet military was not entirely satisfied with the IS-2 heavy tank, especially the early variants, because it was not completely protected against 75mm and especially 88mm German anti-tank and tank guns. For this reason, IS-2 upgrades were launched in the spring of 1944, aimed primarily at reinforcing the tank’s armor. The work was simultaneously conducted at two plants – Chelyabinsk Plant and Experimental Factory No.100 – each of which was developing a different variant of the upgrade. By December 1943, both projects were nearing completion. The newly forged hulls were being tested under fire, and both designs proved to have advantages. The decision was made to create a vehicle whose armor would combine the benefits of both projects. In particular, the dual-sloped hull front was taken from the Experimental Factory No.100 design, and the bottom was taken from the tank made at the Chelyabinsk Plant. The turret design combined elements from both projects. This new tank was designated the IS-3.
In the spring of 1945, the new tank entered field tests, which it passed successfully. Mass production of the IS-3 was launched, but the tank would never be deployed in the Great Patriotic War – by May 1, 1945, only 25 units had been constructed. On September 7, 1945, IS-3 tanks took part in the Allied Victory parade in Berlin, dedicated to the end of the World War II. They made a strong impression on the allies.
The IS-3 never took part in the Korean War, but if the conflict had escalated - had there been direct encounters between Soviet and US forces -- then these tanks would have been put to use at once. The IS-3 considerably surpassed the M46, the most powerful US tank at the time, in terms of both armaments and armor. In most cases the gun of the IS-3 could destroy a M46, while the gun of the latter could hardly penetrate IS-3’s frontal armor. The drawbacks of the IS-3, inherited from the IS-2, were its low rate of fire, small ammo capacity, and low unit power leading to limited vehicle mobility.
Late in the 1950s, the IS-3 was upgraded and re-designated the IS-3M. About one hundred of these tanks were sold to Egypt to be used against the Israeli military. In addition, IS-3M tanks were also shipped to North Korea and Cuba.
IS-3М tanks served the Soviet Army for quite some time, in particular as gun emplacements along the Chinese border. Their withdrawal order was issued only in 1993.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/rus/T_54]
Т-54 M1949 Medium Tank

The Т-44, a new medium tank featuring improved armor, entered mass production in 1944. However, the military were not fully satisfied with its specifications, and recommended that both the tank’s armaments and armor be improved, to surpass the latest German vehicles. In December 1944, the Т-44B project was introduced, featuring reinforced suspension, better armor and a 100mm gun. In addition, the new tank was to have a more powerful engine, new transmission, and various other improvements. When it was determined that pursuing these upgrades would require nearly as much funds as developing a new tank from a scratch, the project was renamed the T-54. Construction was fast-paced, and already in the spring of 1945 an experimental vehicle entered tests. However, these tests had exposed a number of drawbacks, and with the end of the war, tank requirements changed. Aside from high combat capability, vehicles were to provide reliable and efficient service during peacetime – and wartime tanks are not necessarily built for reliability and efficiency. As a result, instead of entering mass production, the T-54 entered a long remodeling phase, during which time a new turret was developed. The tank was finally commissioned in April 1946, and mass production was launched only in 1947. However, the first vehicles off the line were inadequate, and so mass production was halted in 1949 while the exposed issues were corrected. Yet another turret was developed, the design further modified, and mass production resumed. Continued modernization never ceased, which resulted in numerous tank variations.
It its day, the Т-54 combined reliable protection and powerful armaments with moderate weight, surpassing all enemy tanks in its class. The tank's drawbacks included a relatively small ammo capacity and relatively weak gun mask armor. However, the latter was quite small, making it rather difficult to hit.
Being the Soviet’s newest tank, the T-54 did not fight in the Korean War. However, if the conflict had escalated - had there been direct encounters between Soviet and US forces - then these tanks would have given the United States a most unpleasant surprise.
Several Т-54 variants were produced over its 30 year lifetime. According to estimates, the total number of these tanks may have reached about 100 thousand units, making the T-54 the most mass-produced tank in history. This tank was and still is adopted by dozens of countries. Its military history covers most of the wars and armed conflicts of the second half of the 20th century.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/usa/M3_(Lee)]
М3 Medium Tank

The American M3 Medium tank was a further development of the M2 medium tank. By late 1940, the progress of the war in Europe clearly showed that a 37mm gun was no longer an effective tank weapon. The most logical decision was to reequip existing tanks with 75mm guns. But that would require a drastic redesign of the turret, which would take some time, due to US engineers having no prior experience creating turrets for weapons of this caliber. However, the US did not want to wait until the redesign was completed, and wanted to continue producing tanks in the interim. As a result, a very unusual design was proposed, a redesigned M2 tank with a 75mm gun in an archaic sponson mounting, and still retaining its original 37mm gun in the main turret. The resulting tank looked unusual to say the least. However, despite the design’s clear shortcomings, a prototype was ordered in June of 1940. Several large tank factories were also being built at the time. First prototypes of the new M3 tanks were ready for testing by the spring of 1941. In August, serial production began at five factories simultaneously.

The tank was of a very unusual design, with the armament positioned at three levels. The first level consisted of the 75mm gun in the right sponson and two fixed .30-cal machine guns in the front armor plate. The second level was the turret with a 37mm gun and a coaxial .30-cal machine gun. The final level was a rotating commander’s cupola with its own .30-cal. Both the 37mm and 75mm guns were stabilized in elevation only. The tank crew consisted of six men.

The tank’s unique appearance led many observers to immediately form a negative opinion. However, other than a limited coverage for the 75mm gun, the only other shortcoming was its tall silhouette. The tank’s armor was superior to German tanks of 1941-1942, although it was slightly worse than that of the Russian T-34. The M3’s armament was extremely powerful for its time period. Two stabilized main guns with excellent ballistic performance could confidently engage all Axis tanks. The 75mm gun could penetrate a Tiger tank’s side armor. The tank was reliable and durable, comfortable for the crew, and both the driver and commander had good visibility.

Several variants of the M3 were produced, differing in hull manufacturing method (riveted, cast, welded), and engine type (gasoline and diesel). A variant specifically for the British had a cast turret without the cupola, designated Grant I. By the time production ended in December of 1942, 6,230 M3 tanks of all variants were produced, including 1,685 Grant Is.

M3 saw action on all fronts. Both the British Grants, and the American M3s, which the British called “Lee”, served in North Africa, where they were well respected by the crews, thanks to the powerful armament. According to some data, M3s also saw action with the Polish units in 1944 in France. M3s saw action in Burma, and other Pacific locations, both with US and Australian forces. After landing in Africa in November of 1942, US forces were mainly equipped with the M4 Sherman and the light M3 Stuart, except for the 1st Armored Division, which was mainly equipped with the medium M3 Lee/Grant. With the Old Ironsides, the M3 was involved in heavy fighting in Tunisia, including the Battle of Kasserine Pass, where the 1st suffered heavy losses. By May of 1943 the division still had fifty-one M3s operational.

The USSR received 976 M3s in 1942-1943 via the lend-lease program, redesignating the tank M3s (for Sredniy, or Medium). With Soviet tank brigades and separate tank regiments the M3 saw a lot of action in 1942-1943, participating in the Battle of Kursk and continuing to fight sporadically in 1944. By July 4th of 1943 the Voronezh front had 75 M3s tanks, 31 of them with the 192nd Tank Brigade, 32 with 230th Separate Tank Regiment, and 12 with the 245th STR. The 245th took the brunt of the German attack, and by July 11th its M3s and M3l tanks took part in the Battle of Prokhorovka.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/usa/M4_(Sherman)]
М4 Sherman Medium Tank

The M3 tank, due to its unusual design, was clearly an interim solution. Soon after the work on the M3 commenced, the design of a completely new tank began. The vehicle was envisioned as the continuation of the M3 design, addressing the predecessor’s shortcomings. Large-scale design work began on February 1st of 1941, and by June a wooden mock-up was complete, which was designated T6. By the 2nd of September, 1941 a prototype was ready, which differed from later production examples in several details, including a side access hatch and a commander’s cupola. Very quickly, by September 5h, the tank was recommended for production. The first serial run commenced in November. On December 11th, 1941 the tank was accepted for service as the M4 Medium Tank, and dubbed General Sherman after a Civil War Union general. The first major variant of the tank, M4A1, went into mass production in February of 1942.

The M4 was a tank of standard design with front transmission housing. The frontal section of the hull was well sloped at 56 degrees, making it quite capable of stopping all contemporary shells; but the side armor was vertical. The hull compartment contained the driver and his assistant, who also fired the ball mount machine gun. The turret housed the commander, gunner, and loader. The tank’s armament consisted of a 75mm M3 gun stabilized in elevation only, as well as a .50-cal machine gun in a flexible AA mount and two .30-cal machine guns (coaxial and in right bow). Several variants of the tank existed, differing in hull manufacturing method (cast or welded), engine type (three gasoline and two diesel types), suspension (late-war variants even had a wide-tracked “horizontal” HVSS suspension), and armament, with many late-war examples housing a more powerful 76mm main gun. In addition to that, examples with the same formal designation produced at different factories or at different times could also differ in some details.

The Sherman became the workhorse US battle tank, like the T-34 with the Russians. The Sherman was designed with mass production in mind, which made it easy to set up manufacture at factories that never dealt with military hardware before. When properly cared for, the vehicle was very reliable, and required minimal maintenance. The tank also offered comfortable crew arrangements. Crew survivability was gradually improved by moving fuel tanks into separate compartments, and later moving ammo stowage into separate water-filled jackets in the hull floor. The tank had a superb radio transmitter, excellent sights, and a stabilized main gun (an exclusive feature of US-made tanks at the time). The main gun had a low muzzle velocity, due to its design originating with an ancient Mle1897 French division gun. However, great quality of ammunition led to better overall effectiveness compared to the newer Soviet F-34 gun. Unfortunately, when engaging German heavy tanks such as the Tiger, or the frontal armor of later Panzer IV variants, the 75mm gun was clearly inadequate. The situation improved only with the installation of the 76mm M1A1 gun; however these variants became available en masse only very late in the war. Another serious disadvantage was the lack of an APCR shell. The tank’s armor was quite good, generally equal to those of the Pz.IV and the T-34. Another disadvantage of the Sherman was its high silhouette, which made it quite vulnerable.

Tanks of the initial M4 variant had a welded hull and a Continental R-975 gasoline engine. This variant was designed for production at factories with no equipment to cast iron, and as such, serial production began in July 1942, later than the M4A1. M4s served with the 1st and 3rd US Armored Divisions, which were a part of the force that landed in North Africa in November of 1942. The Sherman was the most numerous US tank in Tunisia, and generally performed well, although the inexperience of crews and field commanders often led to unnecessary losses. Subsequently, Sherman tanks saw action in Italy, Western Europe, and the Pacific. 2096 M4 Sherman tanks were sent to the British, which rearmed it with a powerful 17-pdr gun, a modification designated Sherman Firefly. A total of 6,748 Sherman tanks of the M4 variant were produced between July of 1942 and January of 1944, and an additional 1,641 M4(105) variants armed with a 105mm howitzer were produced between February of 1944 and March of 1945.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/usa/M4a1_(Sherman)]
М4А1 Sherman Medium Tank

The M4A1 was the first variant of the Sherman to go into production in February of 1942, and the first Sherman to see action. This model differed from the stock M4 by having a cast upper hull. This detail made manufacturing much quicker, but it also led to the tank being somewhat less survivable, especially with the first production series that had problems with armor quality. The tank was powered by a Continental R-975 gasoline engine. 

The first Sherman tanks saw action not with the US Army, but with the British. In September of 1941, brand new Sherman tanks were delivered to North Africa via the lend-lease program, including M4A1s and some M4A2s. By late October, the new tanks, finally accepted by the British after some changes, saw action at the Second Battle of El Alamein, helping the British attain a decisive victory. The Sherman first saw combat with the US Army in Tunisia in December of 1942. Large numbers of Sherman tanks were used in fighting for the Faid pass and the Kasserine pass; in the latter battle it suffered heavy losses, due to the US having little experience with armored warfare, and the Germans using the heavy Tiger tanks, against which early Sherman tanks could do very little. Subsequently, the M4A1 saw action in Italy, Europe, and the Pacific. 942 M4A1s were sent to the British, which rearmed it with a powerful 17-pdr gun, a modification designated Sherman Firefly. 1990 were sent to Russia via the lend-lease program, where the Sherman was well-liked by its crews.

A total of 6,281 M4A1s were produced between February of 1942 and December of 1943, followed by an additional 3,426 M4A176(W) variants, armed with a 76mm gun. Additionally, Canadians set up their own production of the Sherman, manufacturing 188 M4A1s in late 1942 – early 1943, designated Grizzly I. 

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/usa/m4a2_(sherman)]
М4А2 Sherman Medium Tank

In April 1942 serial production of the M4A2 Medium Tank was launched. Unlike the M4A1, the hull of the M4A2 was fully cast, not welded. Compared to the M4A1 the frontal armor was reinforced.  From other M4 Medium Tanks the M4A2 with a welded hull differed by a slightly altered shape of the rear body and the design of the mufflers. The M4A2 Medium Tank was the only vehicle in the family equipped with a diesel engine: the GMC 6046. Later versions of the tank received an all-cast lid of the transmission compartment previously made of three bolted parts, a changed front hull plate and also new hatches for the driver and co-driver. The number of the M4A2 Medium Tanks produced totals 8,053.  

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/usa/m4a2_76_w_(sherman)]
М4А2(76)W Sherman Medium Tank

In May 1944, Fisher Tank Arsenal began to manufacture a medium tank M4A2 equipped with a 76 mm (М4А2(76)W) gun. 2,894 tanks of this type were produced by May 1945. Another 21 tanks were manufactured by Pressed Steel Co.
The M4A2(76)W Medium Tanks with 76 mm gun received a turret of a different shape featuring a commander’s cupola. From August 1944 the design was enhanced with a round double hatch for the loader.
A power plant with 2 parallel twinned 6-cylinder GMC 6046 was installed with an output of 375 hp at 2,300 rpm. The tank was equipped with a powerful 76 mm М1А1 gun (model М1А2 with muzzle brake) with an AP shell muzzle velocity of 810 m/s. Installation of this gun enabled the Shermans to combat heavy German tanks.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/usa/m4a3_76_w_(sherman)]
М4А3(76)W Sherman Medium Tank

The campaigns in Tunisia and Italy revealed that the 75 mm M3 gun lacked the capability to efficiently fight the newest German tanks Pz.Kpfw.V and Pz.Kpfw.VI as well as the latest versions of the Pz.Kpfw.IV. There was a need for a new modification of the M4 Medium tank equipped with a more powerful gun. The 76 mm M1 gun and its conversions, the M1A1 and the M1A2, were designed to meet these requirements. Aside from the gun, the ‘long-barrel’ M4 differed from the basic variant by the T23 turrets. Tanks equipped with T23 turrets and 76 mm guns were designated (76)W. The first tank to enter serial production in January 1944 was the M4A1(76)W. Production of the M4A3(76)W (called Sherman IVA in the British Army) began in March of the same year. The first 1,400 tanks carried the VVSS suspension; on the rest of the machines the HVSS suspension system was installed. A total of 4,542 units of this type were manufactured. 
From a distance of 1km an armor-piercing shell of the 76 mm M1A1 gun could penetrate 88 mm tick armor plates, which enabled the tank to combat German Pz.Kpfw.V Panther tanks. Additionally, HVAP sub-caliber shells were available for this gun, too, capable of penetrating 132 mm armor from the same distance or 99 mm armor from 2 km. Thus the advent of the M4A3(76)W and other M4 equipped with a 76 mm gun marked the arrival of a worthy rival for the German ‘steel beasts’.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/usa/m4_(sherman)_105us]
М4А3(105) Sherman Medium Tank with 105-mm howitzer M4

This variant of the M4 Sherman medium tank was armed with a 105 mm M4 howitzer, which provided it with more powerful weapons to fight unarmored targets.

The 105 mm M4 howitzer was a modified M2A1 howitzer for use in the tank. M4 rifled howitzer, 105 mm caliber, barrel length 24.5 caliber, rifling pitch 20 calibers. Sliding shutter, unitary loading. The howitzer is mounted in an M52 mask-mount, the ammunition is 66 rounds, and is placed in the right sponson (21 shots), as well as on the floor of the fighting compartment (45 shots). Two more shots were stored directly in the tower. The tower does not have a basket, since the latter makes it difficult to access the ammunition. Due to difficulties with balancing the gun, there is no stabilizer, in addition, the turret does not have a hydraulic drive (it was returned to some tanks in the summer of 1945).

The 105-mm howitzer was armed with tanks of the M4 and M4A3 modifications, while the index "(105)" in the USA and "B" in England were added to their designation. A total of 1,641 M4(105) (out of 8,389 cars of this modification) and 3,039 M4A3(105) (out of 11,424 cars of this modification) were produced. The first production tank with a 105-mm howitzer was accepted from the factory in February 1944. However, only tanks produced on the basis of the M4 (Sherman IB) got to the UK in the amount of 593 units.

Sherman tanks with a 105-mm howitzer were used for direct fire support and smoke screens as part of platoons of "Assault Guns" from six vehicles belonging to tank battalions. Armed with a 105-mm howitzer, the vehicles were limited in actions against enemy tanks due to the weak armor-piercing qualities of the howitzer, which was not designed to fight tanks.

[EncDescription_data/Units/Tanks/Usa/M3A1_afrika]
М3А1 Stuart Light Tank

The American M3A1 Stuart light tank entered production in June of 1942. It was a further modification of the M3 Light Tank. The main changes included the deletion of the commander’s cupola, changes to the turret shape and turret traverse mechanism, and an installation of a gun stabilizer.  During the production run some additional changes were made; early production variants had a riveted hull with sponson machine guns, and later examples had a welded hull with machine guns deleted. Most examples were powered by the powerful Continental-Wright radial engine, with 211 M3A1s having diesel engines.

The tank was ideally suited for recon duties, or pursuit and harassment of retreating enemy forces, possessing great driving performance and reliability. At the same time, attempts for front-line use were usually unsuccessful. The armament and armor of the Stuart were inadequate for such a role. By all other parameters, the tank was superior to comparable Allied tanks, such as the Soviet T-70, due to its superior speed and better defined crew roles; and it was greatly superior to the German Panzer II. 

Geographically, the use of M3A1 spanned the entire globe. US armed forces used the Stuart in the Mediterranean and the Pacific. For example, the 1st Armored Division, upon landing in North Africa in November of 1942, had 158 M3 and M3A1 light tanks. However, first engagements with German panzers quickly exposed the tank’s shortcomings, making it clear that it had little chance in the field. Especially heavy losses were suffered at the Battle of the Kasserine Pass. When used by the British, lend-lease M3A1s (designated Stuart III) fought in North Africa starting in November of 1941, and saw action in Burma and in Normandy. Soviet Union received 1,232 M3 and M3A1 tanks between January of 1942 and April of 1943, most of them the latter model. Most of the units had gasoline engines, although some had diesel. In the USSR, the tanks were designated M3l, to differentiate them from the M3 Lee/Grant Medium Tank, which the Russians redesignated M3s. Both the M3l and M3s were used alongside each other in tank brigades and separate tank regiments. Most of these tanks fought with the Transcaucasian Front. The brunt of the fighting for the tank took place in 1942, some also happening in 1943, and individual models taking part in combat even later. In the course of the Battle of Kursk, by July 4th, 1943 the Voronezh front had 51 M3l tanks, 24 of them with the 192nd Tank Brigade, and 12 with the 245th Separate Tank Regiment. The 245th took the brunt of the German attack, and by July 11th its M3s and M3l tanks took part in the Battle of Prokhorovka.

By the time serial production of the M3A1 ended in early 1943, a total of 4,621 were built. The M3A1 was then replaced by the next variant, the M3A3.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/usa/m5a1_(stuart)]
М5А1 Light Tank

The M5/M5A1 Light Tank was designed by the Cadillac Co. In October 1941 this company – a newcomer to tank engineering  - created its version of the M3A1 Light Tank with the key difference being a power plant consisting of twin Cadillac V8 automobile engines. The design with the designation M3A2E proved to be a success and entered service under the name M4 Light Tank. Although this tank was never put into mass production it became the basis for a more advanced tank, whose first models rolled off the assembly line in December 1942.
The tank officially referred to as M5 Light Tank differed substantially from the M3/M3A1 Light Tank line. Its hull was redesigned from scratch and only its running gear remained the same. Its shape slightly resembled the M3A3 Light Tank but unlike the predecessor the side plates were not sloped but positioned vertically. The tank was equipped with the twin-engine Cadillac V8В engine, proven on the M3A3E model. The turret was borrowed from the M3A1 Light Tank. A total of 2,074 M5 Light Tanks were manufactured. After short time already the M5 Light Tank was replaced on the assembly line by a more advanced model, the M5A1. It featured a different turret borrowed from the M3A3 Light Tank. In late M5A1 models the anti-aircraft machine gun was hidden in a special armored casing in the right-hand part of the turret. The M5A1 became the most mass produced version in the whole M3/M5 Light Tank family – some 6,810 tanks of this type were manufactured.
Although the first tanks of the M5/M5A1 family were manufactured already at the end of 1942 until the beginning of 1943, they were not used for combat until early 1944. By that time the 37 mm M6 gun was obviously out-dated and could serve for fighting enemy light-armored vehicles only. It was the gun which seriously limited the combat characteristics of the tank, even though its armor was far from being the thinnest (the front plate was 60 mm thick). Apart from the US Army, the M5/M5A1 Light Tanks were also used by the British. It is interesting that in the UK tanks of both modifications received the same designation, Stuart VI. Quite frequently tanks of this type were converted to armored troop carriers or reconnaissance vehicles: the conversion consisted in removing the turret and installing a large-caliber machine gun instead. Also the Howitzer Motor Carriage M8 HMC, also known as Scott, was developed on the basis of the M5/M5A1

[EncDescription_data/Units/Tanks/Usa/M4A3E8]
M4A3(76)W HVSS Medium Tank

The most extensively mass-produced US medium tank of World War II, the M4 Sherman had numerous variants. Some variations resulted from manufacturing plant technological peculiarities, some were upgrades designed to improve the vehicle’s combat abilities.
The M4A3 variant featured a 500hp Ford GAA V-8 carburetor engine, which was based on an aircraft engine. It was the best of all the motors that Sherman tanks ever had (some of which were quite exotic; for example, one variant was powered by 5 radially connected car engines). The motor had entered testing by the end of 1941. Already in the summer of 1942, the first M4A3 tanks were ready for the field.
A variant of the M4A3, with wet ammo stowage, was launched in September 1943. Its ammo was stowed in crates full of water, which significantly reduced the risk of ammo explosion, in the case of incoming enemy fire. These tanks were designated with the W suffix.
The M4A3(76)W, yet another variant, was launched in March 1944, featuring a new long-barreled 76mm gun. Compared with the old 75mm gun, which had relatively low ballistic qualities, the new piece offered improved armor penetration, and greater chances against the heavily armored German vehicles. Also launched in 1944 were the M4A3Е2, which featured reinforced armor, and the M4A3(105) support tank, armed with a 105mm Howitzer.
Finally, August 1944 saw the launch of the M4A3(76)W HVSS, which featured wet ammo stowage, the 76mm gun, and the new Horizontal Volute Spring Suspension. In addition, this variant featured dual wheels and wider tracks. The production of M4A3(76)W HVSS ceased in June of 1945, at which time a total of 1742 units had been built, some of which were also used in Europe. Even after the war came to an end, the M4A3(76)W HVSS continued to serve in the US Army, as the favorite of the M4 variants.
Shermans began fighting in the Korean War in August 1950, when a tank company of the 89th Medium Tank Battalion, urgently outfitted with nearby vehicles, was relocated to the Pusan Perimeter, to attack North Korean forces in the area of Masan. However, their first battle was not very successful: US forces were forced to retreat in the face of 45mm anti-tank guns, after the loss of 8 vehicles. Their first encounter with North Korean T-34-85 tanks was late in September 1950, when the Shermans of the 70th Tank Battalion, advancing from the Incheon beachhead, were suddenly attacked by a dozen North Korean T-34s. Both sides suffered casualties during the ensuing chaos, and most of the T-34-85 tanks were destroyed by infantry armed with Super Bazookas. By the end of 1950, there North Korea possessed a grand total of 679 M4A3(76)W HVSS tanks, which, by the way, were more often called by their manufacturing designation: the M4A3Е8. The number of Shermans decreased as the war progressed, as these tanks were gradually replaced by the newer M46.
The M4A3(76)W HVSS was similar to the T-34-85 in many respects, and shared similar armor. But while the Soviet tank was smaller and had a more powerful gun, the US counterpart had better survivability due to better placed fuel tanks and wet ammo stowage. The latter also offered better observation and crew comfort, a gun stabilizer, and a large-caliber anti-aircraft machine gun, which proved quite useful in urban and mountain warfare.

[EncDescription_data/Units/Tanks/Usa/m24_(chaffee)]
M24 Light Tank

By the end of 1942, having analyzed the performance of their M3 light tanks in North Africa, the US military concluded that their 37mm guns could not inflict sufficient damage on the reinforced armor of German vehicles, a conclusion which was further cemented by the Tunisian Campaign.
A new tank designated the T7 had been in the planning phase since 1941. Over the course of development, what was originally envisioned as a light vehicle gained significant weight and became a medium tank. The T7 never entered mass production, for testing found that it offered no special advantages over the M4, which was already in production. At the same time it was determined that the M3 was incapable of carrying a larger turret, which limited its field capability. As a result, requirements for a new light tank, designated the T24, were drawn up in March of 1943. Chrysler, entrusted with its construction, worked swiftly, and by September 2, 1943, the new tank, which previously existed only as a blueprint, was adopted and entered mass production. The first prototype entered tests on October 15, 1943, and by April 1944, the new tank line was ready for battle. On June 22, 1944, the new tank was assigned the designation M24. As a side note, the British gave the tank the service name “Chaffee”. Mass production of the M24 continued until July 1945, over which time a total of 4731 vehicles were manufactured.
The M24 was a very well-armed for a light tank, carrying a 75mm gun, three machine guns (including one large-caliber anti-aircraft gun) and a 50.8mm smoke-grenade mortar. Initially developed for use on aircraft, the 75mm gun was designed to be light weight, but otherwise had ballistic qualities similar to those of the 75mm guns used on Sherman tanks. It also featured a gun stabilizer and wet ammo stowage, which reduced the risk of ammo explosion in case of the tank came under enemy fire. The tank's armor was relatively weak, and only offered light protection against small-caliber guns and anti-tank rifles.
M24 tanks were actively used in European warfare in 1945, gradually replacing the obsolete M5, and the M24 was the first US tank to fight in the Korean War. As early as the beginning of July 1950, a number of M24 tanks had been urgently re-deployed from Japan. However, from the first encounter, the high vulnerability of the M24 became quickly apparent, for their sides could even be penetrated by anti-tank rifle fire. Nevertheless, and despite heavy casualties, these tanks remained in active service, until the arrival of medium and heavy tanks. From 1951 to the end of the war, the M24 was actively used in Korea for their primary role: reconnaissance. Thanks to the tank's compact dimensions and light weight, these tanks were well-suited to the region's mountainous terrain.
Many M24 tanks were exported to various countries allied with the US. In some cases they remained in service well into the 21st century. In addition to World War II and the Korean War, the M24 fought in Indo-China and Vietnam.
The M24 chassis was used in various military vehicles, including self-propelled artillery and self-propelled anti-aircraft guns.

[EncDescription_data/Units/Tanks/Usa/M46]
M46 Medium Tank

By the end of World War II, the M26 Pershing was considered the most powerful and modern tank in the US arsenal. However, military command was not entirely satisfied with its engine power, which considerably limited tank’s mobility and maneuverability. The US war chest shrank dramatically after the war, and developing a new tank from the beginning would be prohibitively expensive. The decision was made, and the M26 upgrade program officially launched in January of 1948.
The upgrade would involve nearly doubling the tank's power, with the AV-1790 engine, as well as installing the new CD-850 hydromechanical transmission. A more powerful gun was also considered, but ultimately the original gun of the M26 was upgraded with the addition of an ejector and improvements to the muzzle brake and gun sights. Except for the engine transmission compartment, the hull and turret remained unchanged, and the tank's armor also remained the same.
Ten new prototypes, designated the T40, entered production in 1948, and underwent testing in the summer of 1949. But on July 30, 1949, even before the tests were concluded, the tank was renamed the M46 and, in an unprecedented move for US tanks, was assigned a service name: “General Patton.” The first mass-produced tanks of this line were manufactured in September of 1949. By the time production ended in 1951, a total of 1168 tanks had been produced, including 360 variants, known as the M46A1, which looked the same but featured a number of modifications to the engine, electrical circuitry and so on.
The M46 tank was officially categorized as a medium tank, but in fact it fit somewhere between medium and heavy tanks. Its weight was similar to the German Panther, and a bit less than the Soviet IS-2. Compared with the Soviet tanks, the M46 was significantly more powerful than the much lighter T-34-85, but yielded to the newer T-54, which was somewhat better armored and significantly better armed. Being slightly heavier, the Soviet IS-2 heavy tank, not to mention IS-3, had far better armor and even better armaments. The US tank, on the other hand, had a powerful engine and efficient transmission, a large ammo capacity, advanced gun sights and means of observation, as well as a gun stabilizer.
The first M46 tanks arrived in Korea on August 7, 1950, and their number gradually grew, peaking after the trench warfare phase began. Being the newest and most powerful US tank of the time, the Patton established a good reputation for their decent protection against the North Korean anti-tank and tank guns, and for their 90mm guns, which were well capable of cutting through enemy fieldworks and armored vehicles.
The Korean War was the only major event in the short history of M46. Already by 1957, these tanks were withdrawn and replaced by the newer M47 and M48.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/UK/Crusader_II]
A15 VIA Crusader II

The cruising tank "Crusader" was created in 1939 by the Nuffield Mechanisations and Aero Ltd according to a technical task similar to that issued for the tank "Covenant". The prototype of the tank was put to the test on April 9, 1940, and at the end of the same year the tank was adopted by the British Army under the name "Crusader I" and put into serial production. In 1941, after the release of 250 cars, a new modification of the Crusader II went into production, which was distinguished by reinforced armor.

The Crusader tank, according to the British classification, was a cruiser tank, corresponding in weight (19 tons) to medium tanks. Having a powerful engine and a Christie suspension, the tank was distinguished by good mobility. The tank's armament included a 2-pounder (40 mm) cannon and two machine guns, one of which was located in a special rotating turret in the front of the tank (however, in some cases the turret was removed). The gun was distinguished by good ballistic characteristics and good penetration for its caliber; the lack of fragmentation shells can be noted as a disadvantage, which made it difficult to fight with enemy firing points. The tank's reservation was relatively weak, especially in the side projection, although the inclined arrangement of a number of armor plates enhanced their durability.

At the time of its appearance, the Crusader II looked good in its characteristics, approaching the German Pz.IIIJ L/42 and significantly surpassing the Italian tanks. However, with the advent of the Pz.IIIJ L/60 and Pz.IVF2, the weakness of weapons and armor became obvious, which led to the creation of a new modification of the Crusader III. The main problem of the Crusaders was the low operational reliability, especially in comparison with the American tanks supplied under lend-lease.

Crusader II tanks have been very actively used in battles in North Africa since June 1941, becoming the most massive British tanks used in this theater of operations. By 1943, there were already a few of them left, mostly better-armed Crusader III were used.

A total of 1812 Crusader II tanks were produced in 1941-42.


[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/UK/Crusader_IICS]
A15 Mk VIA Crusader IICS

Since the regular 40-mm and 57-mm guns of British tanks did not have fragmentation shells in their ammunition sets, which greatly complicated the fight against enemy firing points, specialized tanks with the CS (Close Support) index were produced for direct support tasks. Such tanks were created on the basis of conventional linear tanks by replacing the standard gun with a 76-mm howitzer. Since the dimensions of the howitzer were close to those of standard guns, no serious alterations of the tank design were required, and the CS tanks almost did not differ from linear tanks externally. The ammunition of the 76-mm howitzer consisted of high-explosive and smoke shells, the disadvantage was the lack of armor-piercing and cumulative shells, which did not allow fighting enemy armored vehicles.

The Crusader IICS tanks were actively used in battles in North Africa, including in Tunisia, where they were part of the squadrons of the 17/21 Uhlan Regiment of the 6th Tank Division along with the Crusader III tanks.


[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/UK/Crusader_III]
A15 Mk VI Crusader III

Strengthening the frontal armor of German tanks to 50-60 mm significantly reduced the effectiveness of the main armament of British tanks of the initial period of the war - a 2-pounder (40 mm) gun, which made it necessary to use more powerful guns. However, it turned out that the 6-pound (57-mm) cannon did not fit into the regular turret, and in mid-1941, the design of a new turret began. At the end of 1941, the new tower was successfully tested, and in the summer of 1942, new tanks called "Crusader III" began to enter the army.

The new gun allowed the Crusader to confidently hit German medium tanks, and at close distances, under favorable conditions, its projectile could also penetrate the Tiger's side armor. The crew of the car was reduced by one person. The security and mobility of the tank have not changed much compared to the previous modification.

The Crusader III tanks have been actively used in North Africa since the autumn of 1942, including in Tunisia, quickly replacing the previous modification vehicles. However, their security and reliability could not be compared with those of the American Sherman, which quickly displaced the Crusader from the parts. By the time the fighting began in Italy, there were few "Crusaders" in the units.

A total of 1,717 tanks of this type were produced in 1942-43.

On the basis of the Crusader, quite successful self-propelled anti-aircraft installations were created, as well as commander tanks and vehicles of advanced artillery observers.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/uk/a15_(valentine_ii)]
Mk III Valentine II Infantry Tank

Design work on what was to become the Valentine tank began early in 1938, with the development starting as a private venture of the Vickers-Armstrong corporation. The tank was a hybrid of sorts between an infantry and a cruiser tank; from the former, the vehicle received formidable armor plating (which however was much weaker than that of the Matilda II), and from the latter it inherited some essential design elements. Initially the War Office showed little enthusiasm for the prototype, for example complaining about a cramped two-man turret. However, the approaching war in Europe caused the army brass to reconsider, and the tank was ordered into production on April 14, 1939. Serial production proved troublesome. First production examples rolled off the production line only in May of 1940, and thus were too late to participate in the Battle of France. The first variant of the tank, Valentine I, was powered by the AEC A189 gasoline engine; only 300-some of these tanks were built. The same year a new Valentine I
I variant was introduced, powered by the AEC A190 diesel engine, as well as a Valentine IV with the US-made GMC 6-71 diesel engine and a slightly different transmission. Their most apparent difference from the Valentine I was an external fuel tank. 

By 1940 and 1941 standards, the Valentine was an adequate tank. The main gun could penetrate most German armor, except for the frontal armor of the Pz.III and Pz.IV; at the same time, Valentine’s armor could withstand multiple hits from German 37mm and early 50mm guns. By 1942 however the situation began to change. Under some conditions newer long-barrel German 50mm guns could and did penetrate Valentine’s armor, and the appearance of the new 75mm German guns made the situation even worse. By mid-1942, German tanks were also undergoing improvements that increased their frontal armor, making them invulnerable to the Valentine’s 40mm gun from the front. The British responded by introducing a three-man turret, and mounting more powerful 57mm and even 75mm guns on the Valentine. Alas, this was done at the expense of side armor reduced from 60mm to 43-50mm. Nevertheless, multiple variants of the Valentine remained in service until late 1944, becoming the most mass-produced British tank of the war. In its category, the Valentine is considered one of the best tanks of the war.

The brunt of the action for the Valentine took place in North Africa in late 1941 – early 1942, when it made up the bulk of the British armored regiments. British tank crews liked the tank’s armor, small size, and especially admired its reliability, which was problematic with most other British tanks at the time. The tank’s shortcomings included a cramped two-man turret and the high workload for the commander, who also had to double as the loader. A common problem with the British 40mm and 57mm guns was the lack of a high-explosive shell.

By late 1942, the 8th Armored Brigade, advancing on Libya and Tunisia from the East, had few Valentines left in service. The bulk of the tanks were US-made M4 Sherman and M3 Grant tanks. At the same time, the 6th Armored Division that landed in North Africa during Operation Torch had a large number of Valentine and Crusader tanks, which saw a lot of combat in Tunisia, including the Battle of the Kasserine Pass in February of 1943. A large number of Valentines of various models were sent to Russia via the lend-lease program, including practically all the Valentines manufactured in Canada. The first Valentines were received in late 1941, and the last in 1944. The Russians generally liked the Valentine, which were especially common with Soviet cavalry regiments. 

A total of 8,275 Valentines of all variants were produced during the war, 1,420 of them in Canada. 3,782 were sent to USSR. Valentine II production numbered 1,590. Several vehicles were created on the Valentine’s chassis, including the Bishop SPA and the Archer self-propelled anti-tank gun.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/uk/Valentine_IX_afrika]
Mk III Valentine IX Infantry Tank

Design work on what was to become the Valentine tank began early in 1938, with the development starting as a private venture of the Vickers-Armstrong corporation. The tank was a hybrid of sorts between an infantry and a cruiser tank; from the former, the vehicle received formidable armor plating (which however was much weaker than that of the Matilda II), and from the latter it inherited some essential design elements. Initially the War Office showed little enthusiasm for the prototype, for example complaining about a cramped two-man turret. However, the approaching war in Europe caused the army brass to reconsider, and the tank was ordered into production on April 14, 1939. Serial production proved troublesome. First production examples rolled off the production line only in May of 1940, and thus were too late to participate in the Battle of France. The first variant of the tank, Valentine I, was powered by the AEC A189 gasoline engine; only 300-some of these tanks were built. The same year a new Valentine I
