By late 1942, the 8th Armored Brigade, advancing on Libya and Tunisia from the East, had few Valentines left in service. The bulk of the tanks were US-made M4 Sherman and M3 Grant tanks. At the same time, the 6th Armored Division that landed in North Africa during Operation Torch had a large number of Valentine and Crusader tanks, which saw a lot of combat in Tunisia, including the Battle of the Kasserine Pass in February of 1943. A large number of Valentines of various models were sent to Russia via the lend-lease program, including practically all the Valentines manufactured in Canada. The first Valentines were received in late 1941, and the last in 1944. The Russians generally liked the Valentine, which were especially common with Soviet cavalry regiments. 

A total of 8,275 Valentines of all variants were produced during the war, 1,420 of them in Canada. 3,782 were sent to USSR. Valentine II production numbered 1,590. Several vehicles were created on the Valentine’s chassis, including the Bishop SPA and the Archer self-propelled anti-tank gun.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/uk/a12_(matilda_ii)]
Mk II Matilda II (A12) Infantry Tank

The colossal failure of the Mk I Matilda did not come as a surprise to British General Staff.  Even before the war they realized a replacement would be needed, and quickly.  Therefore, the Mk. II Matilda II was unveiled in 1938 as a faster, more heavily armed, and thicker armored version of it's previous version. 

Although only 24 of these tanks were in operation in France in 1940 they posed a huge problem for German forces.  Their thick armor couldn't be pierced by their anti-tank weapons or any of the German tanks of the day.  Only the 88mm anti-aircraft guns had the capacity to puncture and disable the tank.  On the other hand, the 2 pound cannon on this tank could pierce the armor of any German tank in battle at that time.

The Mk II Matilda II's last days for British forces came in North Africa in 1942.  The open terrain gave the Afrikakorps a significant advantage in using the 3.5in anti-aircraft guns.  Attempts to mount a 6 pound gun on this tank failed due to it's small turret ring.  It spent the rest of it's life with the British Forces as a mine sweeper and other specialty duties until production ceased in 1943.

Most of these tanks ended up in the Red Army as part of the Soviet Lend-Lease program.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/uk/a22f_(churchill_iv)_afrika]
Infantry Tank Mk IV (A22) Churchill NA 75

Churchill III / IV with upgraded weaponry using the turret and mantlet from a destroyed or scrapped Sherman (known as NA 75 from North Africa where the conversions took place), or having their current gun rebored to 75 mm (III* / IV (75 mm) ) (84 rounds). More IVs were modified than IIIs, and their performance is virtually identical to the VI. To fit the Sherman mantlet required cutting away the front of the Churchill turret before it was welded in place, then the mantlet slot had to be cut away to give sufficient elevation. The Sherman 75 mm gun was designed for a left hand loader and the Churchill in common with British practice had a right hand loader. The gun was therefore turned upside down and the firing controls adapted. 

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/uk/Cruiser_Tank_Mk_VIII_Challenger_Mk_I]
Cruiser Tank Challenger MkI

In May of 1942, Birmingham Carriage & Wagon Company began development of a tank destroyer based on the Cruiser Tank Mk.VIII Cromwell. The clear choice for the primary gun was the 76 mm 17-pounder OQF Mk.II, a conversion of the 17-pounder antitank gun. The new gun would feature a large breech block, which resulted in a much larger turret than the Cromwell, and also the tank’s hull was also lengthened. A slight modification was made to the chassis, and a sixth bogie wheel was added. The resulting model was designated the Cruiser Tank Mk.I Challenger, of which 200 units were produced.

The Challenger was not a tank in traditional terms. In fact it was a specialized version of a tank destroyer, which differed from the German Jagdpanzer IV/70 and Jagdpanther only by the presence of a turret. The small number of units manufactured speaks to the fact that the design was far from perfect. A much simpler design was to equip M4 Sherman tanks with the same 17-pounder gun, which is precisely what the British did in the end, with their famous Sherman Firefly tank.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/uk/a22f_(churchill_vii)]
Infantry Tank Churchill VII

In December of 1939, the Harland and Wolff company received a contract for the design of a new tank to replace the Matilda II. Specifications proposed by the British General Staff were based on experiences derived from World War I, and therefore it was recommended to redesign the famous British ‘diamond-shaped’ tanks with tracks running around the hull. The only difference was that now the turret and the front hull superstructure, and not the side sponsons, were supposed to carry armament. 

By summer of 1940 a prototype of the tank was designed, designated the A20. Tests showed that the version had numerous failings, but the company Vauxhall continued working on this ‘trench warfare tank’, and in June of 1941 the first series was manufactured. It received the designation Tank, Infantry, Mk.IV Churchill and the specification number A22. The first models of the Tank, Infantry, Mk.IV had numerous faults and frequently broke down, which inspired a remarkable quote by Winston Churchill: "this tank has even more flaws than myself!" The Churchill VII became the best model of the Tank, Infantry, Mk.IV, being also the heaviest and best protected of all British tanks. Some 5,640 units of this family of tanks were manufactured.

The Churchill was a controversial tank. Designed for positional warfare, its only real advantage was its thick armour. The tracks running over the entire hull suggested good off-road capability, especially when faced with obstacles such as trenches. However, these tracks were also highly vulnerable. The extremely high position of the tracks greatly limited to the field of view of the co-driver and the gunner. In any case, the Churchill VII was the only tank mass-produced by the British which fully justified its classification as an ‘infantry’ tank. The standard ammunition load for its 75 mm ROQF Mk.V gun included high-explosive as well as armour-piercing shells. 

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/uk/Infantry_Tank_Mk_IV_(Churchill_VII)_AVRE]
Infantry Tank Churchill Mk.IV AVRE (Armoured Vehicle Royal Engineers)

The armored vehicle of the Royal engineers "Churchill" (Churchill Armored Vehicle Royal Engineers (AVRE)) was a serious modification of the tanks "Churchill III" or "Churchill IV". Crew consisted of six people (the crew of the tank consisting of one driver and transported sappers). In place of the main gun, a muzzle-loading 290-mm mortar "Petard" was installed, which fired 40-pound (~18 kg) bombs, nicknamed "Flying dustbins". Each such bomb had a bursting charge of 28 pounds (~ 13 kg). The effective range of the bomb was 100 yards (90 m). This munition was designed to destroy fortifications, including dugouts and concrete structures. The loader had to stick his head and torso out of the hatch in the hull to load the mortar.

The armored engineering vehicle "Churchill" was created after the unsuccessful landing in Dieppe (1942), when the road to the landed tanks "Churchill" was blocked by a dam separating the beach from the city, and concrete gouges on the streets of the city. It could be equipped with a knife-type, striker-type and roller-type minesweeper, fascine bundles, a ribbon carpet stacker, elongated charges for clearing passages in wire barriers (the so-called Bangalore torpedos), as well as manually installed demolition charges.

After the WWII., the armored engineering vehicles "Churchill" were re-equipped with a 165-mm breech-loading cannon with a low initial velocity of the projectile.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/uk/a27m_(cromwell_iv)]
Cruiser Tank Cromwell MkIV

Development of the Cruiser Tank Mk.VIII Cromwell began in January 1941, with the approval of a design which would replace the Cruiser Tank Mk.VI Crusader. The following January, the prototype received the designation A24 and the name Cruiser Tank Mk.VII Cavalier. However, field trials proved that the machine, and especially its engine -- a modification of the Liberty aircraft engine -- were far from reliable. But on the basis of the failed Cavalier, there was designed a new Cruiser Tank, the Mk.VIII Centaur (А27L), which was to be an improved version of its predecessor. Ultimately, a total of 3,134 Centaur tanks were manufactured, though they hardly saw combat action in their original design.

In January of 1943, the first tanks belonging to the Cruiser Tank Mk.VIII Cromwell family left the workshops of Birmingham Railway Carriage And Wagon Company. They were the Cruiser Tanks Mk.VIII Centaur equipped with Rolls Royce Meteor engines. Fortunately, unlike their prototypes, the new units received no complaints on reliability, and became the main cruiser tanks used by British troops from 1944 to 1945. The first vehicles which would later receive the name Cromwell I were equipped with 6-pounder guns. The main variant, the Cromwell IV, was armed with a 75 mm ROQF Mk. V gun. A total of 1,070 tanks of this type were manufactured, and some Cruiser Tanks Mk.VIII Centaurs were upgraded to become Cromwell standard tanks.

The Cromwell IV became the first ‘cruiser’ tank, which proved to be a real success in the field. Consistent with the design of a standard cruiser tank, their main advantage was not armour but manoeuvrability. The 75 mm gun was powerful enough to disable a Pz.Kpfw.IV tank, but the Panthers -- and even more so the Tigers -- were extremely dangerous rivals for the Cromwell IV. In general, the Cruiser Tank Mk.VIII Cromwell proved to be a fairly good medium tank, and upgrades to its design gave birth to the Cruiser Tank Comet, the finest example of British wartime tank engineering.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/uk/a30_(challenger_i)]
Cruiser Tank Challenger MkI

In May of 1942, Birmingham Carriage & Wagon Company began development of a tank destroyer based on the Cruiser Tank Mk.VIII Cromwell. The clear choice for the primary gun was the 76 mm 17-pounder OQF Mk.II, a conversion of the 17-pounder antitank gun. The new gun would feature a large breech block, which resulted in a much larger turret than the Cromwell, and also the tank’s hull was also lengthened. A slight modification was made to the chassis, and a sixth bogie wheel was added. The resulting model was designated the Cruiser Tank Mk.I Challenger, of which 200 units were produced.

The Challenger was not a tank in traditional terms. In fact it was a specialized version of a tank destroyer, which differed from the German Jagdpanzer IV/70 and Jagdpanther only by the presence of a turret. The small number of units manufactured speaks to the fact that the design was far from perfect. A much simpler design was to equip M4 Sherman tanks with the same 17-pounder gun, which is precisely what the British did in the end, with their famous Sherman Firefly tank.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/uk/a34_(comet_i)]
In May 1943 the company Leyland started developing a new cruiser tank. It was based on the design of the Tank, Cruiser, Cromwell, Mk.VIII. The first prototype with the name Tank Comet and the designation number A34 was ready in February 1944. As opposed to the tanks of the Cavalier-Centaur-Cromwell series, the Comet was not an upgrade but an entirely new tank although its design included up to 40 percent Cromwell parts. Its most notable difference was the turret with a mounted 77 mm OQF Mk.II gun. This gun was a conversion from the 17-pounder antitank gun. Serial production of the Tank, Cruiser, Comet began in September 1944, and A total 1,200 units were produced.
Undoubtedly, the Comet was the most successful British tank of World War 2. Although inferior to enemy tanks in armor protection, the Comet enjoyed superiority in maneuverability. An AP shell the from 77mm OQF Mk.II gun could penetrate a 124 mm thick armored sheet from a distance of 1 kilometer. The British nevercould not take full advantage of their new tank as the war came to an end.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/uk/M5A1_(Stuart)]
Cruiser Tank Stuart VI

In October 1941, the Cadillac Company -- a newcomer to tank engineering – created the M5/M5A1 Light Tank. It was a version of the M3A1 Light Tank, the key difference being a power plant consisting of twin Cadillac V8 automobile engines. The design, designated M3A2E, proved a success and entered service as the M4 Light Tank. Although this tank never entered mass production, it served as the basis of a more advanced tank, whose initial models rolled off the assembly line in December of 1942.

The M5 Light Tank differed substantially from the M3/M3A1 Light Tank line. Its hull was completely redesigned, and only its running gear remained the same. In shape it slightly resembled the M3A3 Light Tank, but unlike its predecessor, the side plates were not sloped but positioned vertically. The tank was equipped with a twin-engine Cadillac V8В drive, which had been proven on the M3A3E model. The turret was borrowed from the M3A1 Light Tank. A total of 2074 M5 Light Tanks were manufactured, but after a short time the M5 Light Tank was replaced on the assembly line with a more advanced model, the M5A1. This tank featured a different turret, borrowed from the M3A3 Light Tank. Late M5A1 models stowed an anti-aircraft machine gun in a special armoured casing in the right-hand part of the turret. The M5A1 became the most mass produced version in the whole M3/M5 Light Tank family, with some 6810 tanks of this type manufactured.

The first tanks of the M5/M5A1 family were being produced from the end of 1942 through the beginning of 1943, but they would not see combat until early 1944. By that time, their 37mm M6 gun was obviously out-dated, and could only serve for fighting light-armoured vehicles. The gun was the weakest link in terms of the tank’s combat capability: its armour was sufficient, the front plate being 60 mm thick. Apart from the US Army, M5/M5A1 Light Tanks were also used by the British. Confusingly, UK tanks of both modifications received the same designation, Stuart VI. It was common for tanks of this type to be converted into armoured troop carriers or reconnaissance vehicles: the conversion consisted of removing the turret and installing a large-calibre machine gun in its place. Also of note, the Howitzer Motor Carriage M8 HMC, known as the Scott, was developed on the basis of the M5/M5A1.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/uk/Sherman_I]
Sherman I Cruiser Tank

The M3 tank, due to its unusual design, was clearly an interim solution. Soon after the work on the M3 commenced, the design of a completely new tank began. The vehicle was envisioned as the continuation of the M3 design, addressing the predecessor’s shortcomings. Large-scale design work began on February 1st of 1941, and by June a wooden mock-up was complete, which was designated T6. By the 2nd of September, 1941 a prototype was ready, which differed from later production examples in several details, including a side access hatch and a commander’s cupola. Very quickly, by September 5h, the tank was recommended for production. The first serial run commenced in November. On December 11th, 1941 the tank was accepted for service as the M4 Medium Tank, and dubbed General Sherman after a Civil War Union general. The first major variant of the tank, M4A1, went into mass production in February of 1942.

The M4 was a tank of standard design with front transmission housing. The frontal section of the hull was well sloped at 56 degrees, making it quite capable of stopping all contemporary shells; but the side armor was vertical. The hull compartment contained the driver and his assistant, who also fired the ball mount machine gun. The turret housed the commander, gunner, and loader. The tank’s armament consisted of a 75mm M3 gun stabilized in elevation only, as well as a .50-cal machine gun in a flexible AA mount and two .30-cal machine guns (coaxial and in right bow). Several variants of the tank existed, differing in hull manufacturing method (cast or welded), engine type (three gasoline and two diesel types), suspension (late-war variants even had a wide-tracked “horizontal” HVSS suspension), and armament, with many late-war examples housing a more powerful 76mm main gun. In addition to that, examples with the same formal designation produced at different factories or at different times could also differ in some details.

The Sherman became the workhorse US battle tank, like the T-34 with the Russians. The Sherman was designed with mass production in mind, which made it easy to set up manufacture at factories that never dealt with military hardware before. When properly cared for, the vehicle was very reliable, and required minimal maintenance. The tank also offered comfortable crew arrangements. Crew survivability was gradually improved by moving fuel tanks into separate compartments, and later moving ammo stowage into separate water-filled jackets in the hull floor. The tank had a superb radio transmitter, excellent sights, and a stabilized main gun (an exclusive feature of US-made tanks at the time). The main gun had a low muzzle velocity, due to its design originating with an ancient Mle1897 French division gun. However, great quality of ammunition led to better overall effectiveness compared to the newer Soviet F-34 gun. Unfortunately, when engaging German heavy tanks such as the Tiger, or the frontal armor of later Panzer IV variants, the 75mm gun was clearly inadequate. The situation improved only with the installation of the 76mm M1A1 gun; however these variants became available en masse only very late in the war. Another serious disadvantage was the lack of an APCR shell. The tank’s armor was quite good, generally equal to those of the Pz.IV and the T-34. Another disadvantage of the Sherman was its high silhouette, which made it quite vulnerable.

Tanks of the initial M4 variant had a welded hull and a Continental R-975 gasoline engine. This variant was designed for production at factories with no equipment to cast iron, and as such, serial production began in July 1942, later than the M4A1. M4s served with the 1st and 3rd US Armored Divisions, which were a part of the force that landed in North Africa in November of 1942. The Sherman was the most numerous US tank in Tunisia, and generally performed well, although the inexperience of crews and field commanders often led to unnecessary losses. Subsequently, Sherman tanks saw action in Italy, Western Europe, and the Pacific. 2096 M4 Sherman tanks were sent to the British, which rearmed it with a powerful 17-pdr gun, a modification designated Sherman Firefly. A total of 6,748 Sherman tanks of the M4 variant were produced between July of 1942 and January of 1944, and an additional 1,641 M4(105) variants armed with a 105mm howitzer were produced between February of 1944 and March of 1945.


[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/uk/Sherman_V]
Sherman V Cruiser Tank

The M4 Sherman, the most common US medium tank of World War II, underwent a number of modifications both for increased ease of production and for better combat performance. Many of these followed corresponding modifications to the M3 medium tank, the predecessor of the M4. In particular, the M4A4 was based on the M3A4 tank, which featured a unique Chrysler A57 Multibank power unit consisting of five radially connected 6-cylinder car engines. The resulting 30-cylinder unit boasted 370 horsepower, but required a longer hull. Though exotic, the unit was nevertheless reliable, and enabled the military to tap into the nation’s considerable car engine production capacity for the manufacture of tank engines.

The production of the M4A4 began in June of 1942 and continued through September 1943. A total of 7499 vehicles were manufactured during its run, making the M4A4 the second most common Sherman modification after M4A2 (of which 8053 units were made). Unlike its predecessor, the M4A4 was not significantly modified during its short production run: it received neither a new hull with a one-piece frontal armour plate, a 76mm gun, a larger turret, or wet ammunition stowage.

These rather non-standard vehicles were not favourites among the US Army, so nearly all of the 7167 M4A4 units were lend-leased to Great Britain, which designated them the "Sherman V." Being quite common, these tanks made up the bulk of the British armoured forces that landed in Normandy in July 1944. Sadly, the very first encounters with German tanks proved that the artillery of the Sherman V – a relatively low-powered 75mm gun – could not reliably penetrate the frontal armour plates of Pz. IV, not to mention the even better-armoured Panthers. By contrast, these German tanks could easily deal with the Shermans. This problem was somewhat addressed by the British M4A4 modification, the Sherman Vс Firefly, which equipped the tank with a powerful 17-pounder gun, but these modifications were not made in great number, so the unmodified Shermans served through the last days of the war.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/uk/Sherman_IC]
Sherman Iс Cruiser Tank

Late in 1941, the British army obtained the powerful long-barrelled (75mm) 17-pounder anti-tank gun of exceptional ballistic capability. A tank modification was quickly ordered, and the gun was initially meant to be mounted on a special modification of the Cromwell tank designated the "Challenger." The newly equipped tank was produced, but failed to meet expectations, so only a limited quantity was produced. Better results were achieved when the gun was mounted onto the lend-leased M4 Sherman tanks, which were initially equipped with a relatively low-power 75mm gun. The gun’s good balance allowed mounting it into the current tank turret without making highly significant design changes. All that was required was the introduction of a new gun mantlet, and the removal of the gyroscopic stabilizer, which was not meant for use with a heavier gun. In addition to the gun replacement, the tank underwent other changes at the same time: the radio was moved from the hull to an armoured box behind the turret; a new loader’s hatch was opened in the turret top; the driver’s mate was replaced by additional ammunition; and finally the bow machine gun was removed, as was the gun’s operator.

These changes were applied to all the various M4 modifications. The new gun greatly improved the tank’s performance against well-armoured enemy vehicles. In fact, in Normandy, the Firefly was the only Allied tank capable of punching through well-armoured German vehicles, the widespread Panthers in particular. Unfortunately, the initial number of Fireflies was rather small, and it was only later that its ammunition stores grew to include highly efficient subcalibre rounds. As a result, Allied armoured forces often suffered considerable losses in encounters with German tanks. In time, the Fireflies increased in number, and these vehicles, as well as M4 modifications equipped with 76mm guns, gradually drove older the Shermans out of service.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/uk/Sherman_VC_Firefly]
Sherman Vс Cruiser Tank

Late in 1941, the British army obtained the powerful long-barrelled (75mm) 17-pounder anti-tank gun of exceptional ballistic capability. A tank modification was quickly ordered, and the gun was initially meant to be mounted on a special modification of the Cromwell tank designated the "Challenger." The newly equipped tank was produced, but failed to meet expectations, so only a limited quantity was produced. Better results were achieved when the gun was mounted onto the lend-leased M4 Sherman tanks, which were initially equipped with a relatively low-power 75mm gun. The gun’s good balance allowed mounting it into the current tank turret without making highly significant design changes. All that was required was the introduction of a new gun mantlet, and the removal of the gyroscopic stabilizer, which was not meant for use with a heavier gun. In addition to the gun replacement, the tank underwent other changes at the same time: the radio was moved from the hull to an armoured box behind the turret; a new loader’s hatch was opened in the turret top; the driver’s mate was replaced by additional ammunition; and finally the bow machine gun was removed, as was the gun’s operator.

These changes were applied to all the various M4 modifications, but the M4A4 remained the most popular, given large numbers lend-leased from the U.S. These modified tanks were designated the "Sherman Vс" and received the semi-official nickname "Firefly," the origin of which is unclear (though it could be related to the gun’s flash).

The new gun greatly improved the tank’s performance against well-armoured enemy vehicles. In fact, in Normandy, the Firefly was the only Allied tank capable of punching through well-armoured German vehicles, the widespread Panthers in particular. Unfortunately, the initial number of Fireflies was rather small, and it was only later that its ammunition stores grew to include highly efficient subcalibre rounds. As a result, Allied armoured forces often suffered considerable losses in encounters with German tanks. In time, the Fireflies increased in number, and these vehicles, as well as M4 modifications equipped with 76mm guns, gradually drove older the Shermans out of service.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/uk/M4_(Sherman)_105]
Cruiser tank "Sherman IB" mod. 1942. with 105-mm howitzer M4

This variant of the M4 Sherman medium tank was armed with a 105 mm M4 howitzer, which provided it with more powerful weapons to fight unarmored targets.

The 105 mm M4 howitzer was a modified M2A1 howitzer for use in the tank. M4 rifled howitzer, 105 mm caliber, barrel length 24.5 caliber, rifling pitch 20 calibers. Sliding shutter, unitary loading. The howitzer is mounted in an M52 mask-mount, the ammunition is 66 rounds, and is placed in the right sponson (21 shots), as well as on the floor of the fighting compartment (45 shots). Two more shots were stored directly in the tower. The tower does not have a basket, since the latter makes it difficult to access the ammunition. Due to difficulties with balancing the gun, there is no stabilizer, in addition, the turret does not have a hydraulic drive (it was returned to some tanks in the summer of 1945).

The 105-mm howitzer was armed with tanks of the M4 and M4A3 modifications, while the index "(105)" in the USA and "B" in England were added to their designation. A total of 1,641 M4(105) (out of 8,389 cars of this modification) and 3,039 M4A3(105) (out of 11,424 cars of this modification) were produced. The first production tank with a 105-mm howitzer was accepted from the factory in February 1944. However, only tanks produced on the basis of the M4 (Sherman IB) got to the UK in the amount of 593 units.

Sherman tanks with a 105-mm howitzer were used for direct fire support and smoke screens as part of platoons of "Assault Guns" from six vehicles belonging to tank battalions. Armed with a 105-mm howitzer, the vehicles were limited in actions against enemy tanks due to the weak armor-piercing qualities of the howitzer, which was not designed to fight tanks.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/fr/bataille_b1_bis]
Development of this tank began in 1921. It’s main purpose was supposed to be infantry support. The tank entered service in March 1934 В1. Since 1935 five companies, - Renault, FCM, Schneider, FAMH and АРХ, - were producing these tanks. When production ended on June 15, 1940 about 403 such tanks had been manufactured.

The В1 bis were engaged in combat on the French Front from May to June 1940 where they earned their reputation as invulnerable tanks, since no German tank or antitank gun could ever penetrate their armor.
After France was occupied, the Germans captured about 161 В1 bis tanks in working condition which later received the designation В2 740(f).

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/fr/renault_r35]
In 1934 the company Renault, following an order from the Army, developed the light tank R35. This tank was in serial production from 1935 to 1940. During this period Renault delivered about 1,070 tanks to the French Army and exported some other 560 units.
The Renault R35 became the first version to enter serial production. Later on, a conversion, the R39 with a long-barrel 37 mm SA38 gun was developed. Then a model followed with a new suspension system by AMX and a similar gun to that of the R39. The tank was designated R40.
The French Army used the R35, R39 and R40 tanks in all theatres of operations. In May 1940 a total of 945 units were deployed in Europe, of which 810 served in dedicated tank battalions and 135 were allocated to the 4th Tank Division of the Reserve (DCR).
Several colonial units in North Africa were equipped with R35 tanks. The 68th Tank Battalion was deployed in Syria, the 63rd and 62nd Battalions served in Tunisia and Algeria respectively. R35 were also in service in Poland, Yugoslavia, Romania and Turkey.
After the defeat of France in 1940, about 800 R35 tanks fell into German hands. Germans used most of the tanks for training purposes and some of them, after removing the turret, were rebuilt into artillery tractors for 150 mm howitzers and 210 mm mortars.  
The Renault R35 took part in combat actions in the French 5th Dragoon Regiment during the liberation of France in 1944-1945.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/fr/hotchkiss_h35]
The Hotchkiss, the main French light tank was designed by Hotchkiss in 1934. About 1,000 units were manufactured from 1935 to 1940.
By the beginning of the war in May 1940 the French Army counted some 821 Н35/38/39 vehicles.
The light Н35 tanks served in separate tank battalions assigned to field armies.
In the cavalry, the Н35/38/39 tanks were in service with Mechanized Light Divisions, Cavalry Light Divisions and Mechanized Infantry Divisions. 
The 1st and the 2nd Mechanized Light Divisions counted 40 H35 tanks each. The 1st and the 2nd Cuirassier Regiments forming part of the 3rd Mechanized Light Divisions were equipped with the same number of the Н39 tanks, and 60 H35 units served in the 11th Dragoon Regiment. Four of the five Light Cavalry Divisions in the Armored Cavalry Reconnaissance Regiment had 12 H35 tanks each at their disposal and the fifth counted 12 H39 tanks. 
One tank squadron of 16 H35 was deployed in North Africa. The 342nd and the 351st tank companies equipped with H35 and Н39 tanks saw combat action in Norway (1940).
After France suffered defeat in 1940 about 600 Н35/39 tanks were captured by German troops. The tanks were repaired and modified: German radios were installed and the commander’s cupola was replaced with a double hatch. Under the designation PzKpfw 38H 735 (f) the tank was used in the Wehrmacht and the SS. 

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/fr/renault_amr_35zt]
An improved variant of the AMR 33. Renault Co., the developer of the tank, started its production in 1935. Some 200 units were manufactured before 1940.
The AMR 35ZT was the first mass-produced version. The tank had a riveted hull and turret with sloped armor. 
By September 1939, 129 AMR 35,10 AMR 35ZT2 and AMR 35ZT3 were deployed. They were used in the 1st and the 4the Dragoon Regiments of the 1st and the 2nd Mechanized Cavalry Divisions, 69 machines in each. The 7th (the 1st DIM) and the 6th (the 3rd DIM) Tank Reconnaissance Groups were equipped with four AMR 35 tanks, versions ZT2 and ZT3, each. After France was defeated most tanks of that type were captured by German troops and used by the Wehrmacht as transports for 80 mm mortars.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/fr/renault_ft17]
Until the mid-1930s the French tank fleet was based on the Renault FT-17 tank and its different modifications. It appears to be the first tank in the world with a fully rotating turret. The tank was produced since 1917 by Renault, Berliet, Schneider and Delauney-Belleville. In total some 7,820 units were manufactured.
The hull and the multi-sided turret of the Renault FT-17 were riveted. Some tanks had a ‘tail’ which enabled them to overcome wide ditches and trenches.
By 1940 the French Armored forces had 7 dedicated tank battalions (the 11th, the 18th, the 29th, the 30th, the 31st, the 33rd, the 36th ВСС) equipped mainly with FT-18 tanks, about 63 tanks in each. Also, three dedicated FT-18 companies (the 343rd , the 344th , the 350th Cie.A) were formed having about 10 tanks each. The rest of the tanks of this type were stored in depots.
Before the outbreak of the war, FT-17 (FT-18) tanks were exported to Afghanistan, Belgium, Brazil, Greece, Holland, Spain, Italy, Poland, USA, Finland, Czechoslovakia, Yugoslavia and Japan.
The FT-17 and FT-18 tanks serving in the Polish Army took part in the war against the USSR in 1920 and against Germany in the autumn of 1939. Also, these vehicles fought in France against German troops. However, after the French Campaign was finished, the FT tanks captured by the Germans were used in police units and for guarding airfields, and served under the designation PzKpfw 18R 730 (f).

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/fr/renault_ft31]
FT modifié 31: upgraded tanks with 7.5 mm Reibel machine gun. After trials from 1929 to 1931, this modification was made in 1933–1934 on 1000 chars mitrailleurs still in French stocks. This version was sometimes referred to as the "FT 31", though this was not the official name.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/fr/somua_s35]
The medium tank Somua S35 was designed by the company Somua in 1935 by request of the French Cavalry Command. About 500 tanks were manufactured between 1935 and 1940.
Before 1940, S35 medium tanks were delivered to French units exclusively and never exported to other countries. The Mechanized Cavalry Regiments of three Mechanized Light Divisions were equipped with these tanks. The S35 served in the 4th Cuirassier, the 18th Dragoon, the 13th and the 29th Dragoon, the 1st and the 2nd Cuirassier Regiments. In Tunisia the 6th Light Cavalry Division counted 50 S35 tanks.
After the defeat of France about 300 S35 tanks were captured by the Germans, who reequipped the commander’s cupola with a double hatch and gave the tank the designation PzKpfw 35S 739 (f). Later these tanks were used on the Eastern front in the 211th Tank Battalion and in Crimea in the 204th Tank Regiment. In Normandy, the 100th Tank Reserve Battalion and the 206th Tank Battalion were partly reequipped with tanks of this type and fought against the Allies. In 1941, Germans converted about 60 tanks into artillery tractors.
In 1944 during combat operations in France the  21st Tank Division of the Wehrmacht counted 23 S35 tanks and 17 S35 tanks served in the French Forces of the Interior (FFI), liberating the country.
The S35 tank was a quite menacing rival for German tanks and may be considered the best French tank.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/Ita/Carro_Armato_M13_40]
Carro Armato M13/40

The first "medium" (according to Italian concepts) M11/39 tank can hardly be called a successful machine - its main disadvantages were a weak and poorly placed cannon, as well as insufficient booking. The imperfection of the design of this, in fact experimental, tank was well understood by the Italian military and designers, and its serial production amounted to only 100 vehicles. Immediately after the launch of the M11/39 into mass production, it was decided to begin work on creating a new tank based on it, which became known as the M13/40.

The design of the M13/40 began at the Ansaldo company in December 1937, the prototype of the machine was ready in October 1939, the tank was launched into mass production in June 1940. From the M11/39, the new tank inherited the engine, transmission, suspension and partially the hull structure.

Structurally, the M13/40 was a classic light tank weighing 14 tons (according to the Italian classification, the tank was considered medium). The body of the machine was assembled on rivets, the thickness of the armor was 25-30 mm. The engine is 125 hp diesel. The main armament is a 47-mm cannon with a barrel length of 32 caliber, there were also three or four machine guns (paired, two course and anti-aircraft on a number of vehicles). The crew consisted of 4 people, two of whom were housed in the tower. Compared to foreign tanks of the same class, the M13/40 is hardly an outstanding machine. His gun, which was an anti-tank gun adapted for installation on a tank by the Austrian company Böhler, had a low initial speed and was significantly inferior in armor penetration to analogues. The tank's reservation provided relatively reliable protection only from the fire of anti-tank rifles, heavy machine guns and 20 mm cannons. The tank's engine did not match the weight of the car, which led to its overload and low maximum speed.

When compared with tanks of a similar class of allies - the American slightly lighter M3 Stuart and the two tons heavier British Valentine, the Italian tank clearly loses - the allied tanks are armed with guns with high muzzle velocity, without problems piercing the armor of the M13/40 at real battle distances, while the cannon of the Italian tank could not hit the Valentine at all, and the Stuart was struck only in favorable conditions, for example, when shooting into the side; at the same time, the American tank greatly benefits from the Italian in mobility.
The M13/40 was actively used by the Italian army in the Balkans and North Africa in 1940-43. Compared to the M11/39 and CV3/33 tankettes, tanks of this type proved to be quite good, although the weakness of armament and reservations made themselves felt, especially since 1942, when the British early cruiser tanks, with which the M13/40 could still somehow fight, began to be replaced by "Crusaders", "Stuarts" and "Grants". The battle of El Alamein was the last in which the M13/40 was used in quite large numbers, although a small number of tanks of this type fought in Africa in the future, until the surrender of German-Italian troops in Tunisia in 1943. About two dozen of these tanks fell into the hands of the Germans after the surrender of Italy; in the German army they received the index M13/40 735(i) and were used in combat.

The M13/40 was in serial production in 1940-1941, a total of 710 cars were produced. Instead of the M13/40, its modification M14/41 was put into production, featuring a more powerful engine and a number of minor changes.

A rather successful Semovente da 75/18 self-propelled artillery system was created on the M13/40 chassis, as well as a commander tank without a Carro commando M40 turret.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/Ita/Carro_Armato_M14_41]
Carro Armato M14/41

The revealed overload of the M13/40 tank engine led to the development of a modification of this tank under the M14/41 index with a more powerful 145 hp diesel engine. Other design changes were insignificant - improved filters were installed, ammunition was slightly reduced. In general, the M14/41 retained most of the shortcomings of its predecessor - weak armament and armor, as well as slow speed.

The M14/41 were mass-produced in 1941-1942, a total of 695 tanks of this type were produced. Since 1942, they have been actively used in North Africa, gradually displacing their predecessors; in particular, the Centauro division, known for heavy fighting with its participation in Tunisia, had a large number of tanks of this type. After the surrender of Italy, a small number of tanks of this type (according to some sources, only one car) fell into the hands of the Germans, who used them under the index M14/41 736(i).

On the basis of the M14/41, Semovente da 75/18 and Semovente da 75/34 assault self-propelled guns, Semovente da 90/53 tank destroyers, as well as commander tanks without Carro Commando M41 turrets were created.


[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/Ita/Carro_Armato_M15_42]
Carro Armato M15/42 

The shortcomings of the M13/40 and M14/41 tanks stimulated further work on improving tanks of this type, which led to the creation of the latest, more advanced modification of the M15/42. The new tank was distinguished by the installation of a rifle extended to 40 calibers, a 12 mm thickening of the frontal armor of the turret and the turret box, an increased side kit, as well as the installation of a more powerful (185 hp) gasoline engine and electric drive of the turret.

The new gun produced much better ballistics, which allowed the Italian tank to confidently fight with the American M3 Stuart, and in favorable conditions - with the M4 Sherman. The reservation of the rest was clearly absent, but in some cases it could protect from the fire of 37 mm guns. The mobility of the tank increased significantly, although it was substantially inferior to the same "Stuart", which had a much more powerful engine.
The M15/42 was mass-produced in 1942-43, a total of 220 tanks of this type were produced in the Italian capital, a number of tanks were built later under German control.

A significant number of these tanks fell into the hands of the Germans, who actively exploited them in 1943-45 in Italy, the Balkans, and Hungary.
On the basis of the M15/42, Semovente da 75/18 and Semovente da 75/34 (Scafo M42) assault self-propelled guns were produced, as well as commander tanks without Carro Commando M42 turrets.


[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/Ita/Carro_Armato_L6_40]
Carro Armato L6/40

In 1936, Fiat-Ansaldo began work on a new light tank. Unlike the M11/39-M15/42, which were structurally light tanks, but organizationally occupied the niche of medium tanks, the new vehicle, which received the L6/40 index, was originally created as a light reconnaissance tank, an analogue of the German Pz.II.

Structurally, the L6/40 was a typical light tank weighing 7 tons with a 70 hp gasoline engine with a crew of two people. The car was relatively well armored for its weight - in the frontal projection, the tank was well protected from the fire of anti-tank guns, heavy machine guns and 20 mm cannons. The tank's automatic cannon, created on the basis of the anti-aircraft one, made it possible to successfully fight with armored vehicles and light tanks of the enemy. In general, the L6/40, due to its better gun, was somewhat superior to the lighter Soviet T-60 and slightly (mainly due to a less powerful engine) inferior to the German Pz.IIF.

Serial production of the L6/40 began at the end of 1940 and lasted until 1943, a total of 283 vehicles were produced (in 1944, 17 more tanks were assembled under German control). Tanks of this type got to the front only in 1942. In North Africa, the L6/40 was used in 1942-43 as part of the Lancieri di Navarro and Cavalleggeri di Lodi tank groups. L6/40s were also used on the Eastern Front, in the Balkans and in Italy, both by Italian and German units, who captured a lot of L6/40s and used them under the index Pz. L6/40 733(i).

Based on the L6/40, Semovente da 47/32 light anti-tank self-propelled guns, ammunition transporters, as well as an experimental flamethrower tank were created.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/pol/7tp]
In early 1930s Poland imported 38 Vickers 6-Ton tanks from the UK. The tank’s easily inflammable engine was replaced by an Austrian-made Diesel Saurer. Along with replacing the engine, the tank’s armor protection was reinforced. The armament of the 7ТР included a 37 mm antitank Bofors gun and coaxial 7.92 mm Browning machine gun protected by an armored tube.
In two years about 90 vehicles of this type were manufactured.
In 1938 the tank was improved and the turret was enlarged with a rear compartment that housed a radio and extra ammunition load. The vehicle was equipped with a new device, a semi-gyrocompass that enabled movement in low visibility conditions.
By September 1, 1939 about 152 7TP and similar Vickers 6-Ton tanks were in service in Polish Army. Supported by infantry and artillery those vehicles managed to destroy about 200 German tanks of a total of 2,800 which took part in the Polish campaign.  
Also, the artillery tractor С7Р developed on the base of the 7TP was put into serial production.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/pol/tks]
The British Caden-Loyd MKVI tankette served as the prototype for design of the TK Tankette. In 1929 a department of the Polish Defense Ministry purchased 10 such tankettes from the British company Vickers-Armstrong. 
In 1930, a group of Polish engineers designed and built two models of a high-speed recon tank (TK-1 and TK-2) armed with a 7.92 mm machine gun. But the tankette was not put into serial production because of the lack of armor protection.
Later Polish engineers designed an improved version, the TK-3. In 1931 the first vehicle was assembled at the Ursus plant and soon serial production of the TK-3 began. By the end of 1933 about 300 TK-3 tankettes were produced.
In March 1933 work on a new recon tank was concluded. It was issued to Polish army units as TKS.  The new tankette had a new design and better armor protection.
The TKS Tankette was modified several times during development and production. Before the outbreak of World War II the TKS and TK-3 were rearmed with a 20 mm automatic gun. Also, a series of experiments was conducted with the goal to improve armor protection, replace the power unit and armament. As result dozens of new vehicles based on the TK-3 and the TKS were developed: the TKD with 47 mm gun, the TKW with rotating turret, the TKF with a more powerful engine and better armor protection.
More than 400 TK-3 tankettes and about 260 TKS tankettes were engaged in combat in September 1939. The tankettes were used in special reconnaissance tank companies that were part of infantry divisions. Thin armor and weak armament made these tankettes no rivals to the German Pz. II, Pz. III and Pz. IV tanks.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/pol/tks_mg]
The British Caden-Loyd MKVI tankette served as the prototype for design of the TK Tankette. In 1929 a department of the Polish Defense Ministry purchased 10 such tankettes from the British company Vickers-Armstrong. 
In 1930, a group of Polish engineers designed and built two models of a high-speed recon tank (TK-1 and TK-2) armed with a 7.92 mm machine gun. But the tankette was not put into serial production because of the lack of armor protection.
Later Polish engineers designed an improved version, the TK-3. In 1931 the first vehicle was assembled at the Ursus plant and soon serial production of the TK-3 began. By the end of 1933 about 300 TK-3 tankettes were produced.
In March 1933 work on a new recon tank was concluded. It was issued to Polish army units as TKS.  The new tankette had a new design and better armor protection.
The TKS Tankette was modified several times during development and production. Before the outbreak of World War II the TKS and TK-3 were rearmed with a 20 mm automatic gun. Also, a series of experiments was conducted with the goal to improve armor protection, replace the power unit and armament. As result dozens of new vehicles based on the TK-3 and the TKS were developed: the TKD with 47 mm gun, the TKW with rotating turret, the TKF with a more powerful engine and better armor protection.
More than 400 TK-3 tankettes and about 260 TKS tankettes were engaged in combat in September 1939. The tankettes were used in special reconnaissance tank companies that were part of infantry divisions. Thin armor and weak armament made these tankettes no rivals to the German Pz. II, Pz. III and Pz. IV tanks.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/pol/vickers_e_dw]
In the late 1920s the Polish Army needed a modern home produced tank. Since Poland lacked experience in tank engineering, the decision was made to purchase abroad a license for manufacturing. As a resultthe Vickers Mk.E and 7TP light tanks entered service in the Polish Army.
In 1928 Vickers Armstrong Limited built the first prototype of the 6-ton Light Tank Mark E.  The tank was designed in two versions: the Mk.E – dual-turret, armed with machine guns; and the Mk. E type, the world’s first tank with twin armament, a gun and a machine gun in a single turret (duplex mounting).
The first 6-ton tank passed trials in Poland in September 1930 and in September 1931 the decision was made to purchase 50 machines and license the tank for local production. But Poland never started to manufacture the Vickers E Tank, because Polish designers improved its design and developed a new light tank, the 7TP. The tanks ordered were modified in 1934 and in August of the same year they officially entered the ranks of the Polish Army.
After the Vickers E entered service, a special 3rd Armored Battalion headquartered in Warsaw was created equipped with these tanks. In the late 1930s when the 7TP first entered service, the Vickers tanks were moved to the 2nd Battalion in Zurawica.  Another tank unit equipped with Vickers tanks was the 11th Battalion of the Armored Forces Training Center in Modlin. About 5 tanks in each battalion served as ‘immediate reserve’ and were never used in peacetime. The remaining tanks were actively used for training purposes and hence most of them were heavily worn-out by 1939.

[EncDescription_data/units/tanks/pol/vickers_e_jw]
In the late 1920s the Polish Army needed a modern home produced tank. Since Poland lacked experience in tank engineering, the decision was made to purchase abroad a license for manufacturing. As a resultthe Vickers Mk.E and 7TP light tanks entered service in the Polish Army.
In 1928 Vickers Armstrong Limited built the first prototype of the 6-ton Light Tank Mark E.  The tank was designed in two versions: the Mk.E – dual-turret, armed with machine guns; and the Mk. E type, the world’s first tank with twin armament, a gun and a machine gun in a single turret (duplex mounting).
The first 6-ton tank passed trials in Poland in September 1930 and in September 1931 the decision was made to purchase 50 machines and license the tank for local production. But Poland never started to manufacture the Vickers E Tank, because Polish designers improved its design and developed a new light tank, the 7TP. The tanks ordered were modified in 1934 and in August of the same year they officially entered the ranks of the Polish Army.
After the Vickers E entered service, a special 3rd Armored Battalion headquartered in Warsaw was created equipped with these tanks. In the late 1930s when the 7TP first entered service, the Vickers tanks were moved to the 2nd Battalion in Zurawica.  Another tank unit equipped with Vickers tanks was the 11th Battalion of the Armored Forces Training Center in Modlin. About 5 tanks in each battalion served as ‘immediate reserve’ and were never used in peacetime. The remaining tanks were actively used for training purposes and hence most of them were heavily worn-out by 1939.